00:05:37.571 --> 00:05:38.960
a potential to kind of overcome.

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:44.155
So of course there's an app for
everything, we know this and

00:05:44.156 --> 00:05:48.350
there's been rapid development
of mental health apps.

00:05:48.351 --> 00:05:53.080
So this is kind of based off
a report from the IMS institute

00:05:53.081 --> 00:05:54.260
that came out in 2015.

00:05:54.261 --> 00:05:56.040
I mean, I know that that's
a little outdated now.

00:05:56.041 --> 00:05:59.530
I'll say that when they did
the same report In 2013,

00:05:59.531 --> 00:06:03.830
they estimated there were about
100,000 mental health apps.

00:06:03.831 --> 00:06:06.183
So from 2013 to 2015,

00:06:06.184 --> 00:06:09.990
it jumped by nearly
a little bit more than 50%.

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:15.352
So they estimated in 2015 that
there were roughly 165,000

00:06:15.353 --> 00:06:17.315
health apps that are out there.

00:06:17.316 --> 00:06:19.820
There's a more recent report
from a different institute I saw

00:06:19.821 --> 00:06:23.980
that estimated about 250,000,
which would be keeping up with

00:06:23.981 --> 00:06:26.170
about the rate of development
that we've seen there.

00:06:26.171 --> 00:06:29.690
About roughly 12,000
of those are focused

00:06:29.691 --> 00:06:31.270
towards mental health.

00:06:31.271 --> 00:06:34.014
That's a ton of stuff
that's out there.

00:06:34.015 --> 00:06:37.770
We don't know how good
all these things are.

00:06:37.771 --> 00:06:39.050
We don't know how
helpful they are.

00:06:39.051 --> 00:06:40.860
But there's a lot of different
things out there that

00:06:40.861 --> 00:06:43.550
are purported to be
able to help people for

00:06:43.551 --> 00:06:44.620
different mental
health disorders.

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:49.330
Here's just a little bit
of review of looking at

00:06:49.331 --> 00:06:51.250
the scientific evidence
behind those things.

00:06:51.251 --> 00:06:55.240
These come from two separate
systematic reviews.

00:06:55.241 --> 00:06:59.250
Where what the office did in
both of these papers is they

00:06:59.251 --> 00:07:02.000
looked at all the apps that were
publicly available on the app

00:07:02.001 --> 00:07:03.950
stores focused on
a particular problem.

00:07:03.951 --> 00:07:06.610
So the top one focuses
on depression,

00:07:06.611 --> 00:07:09.360
this lower one
focuses on anxiety.

00:07:09.361 --> 00:07:13.080
The findings are relatively
similar in that, for

00:07:13.081 --> 00:07:15.100
depression they found 117 apps.

00:07:15.101 --> 00:07:18.200
For anxiety they found 316,
about 10% of

00:07:18.201 --> 00:07:21.290
those were consistent with
evidence based practices.

00:07:21.291 --> 00:07:24.520
And by that I mean, they went to
the content of the applications.

00:07:24.521 --> 00:07:25.890
They looked what was in there.

00:07:25.891 --> 00:07:26.960
And they saw,

00:07:26.961 --> 00:07:30.100
does this coincide with what
someone would recommend people

00:07:30.101 --> 00:07:33.210
to do from sort of evidence
based psychological practices.

00:07:33.211 --> 00:07:36.330
So about 10% of the time,
those abstracts are consistent.

00:07:36.331 --> 00:07:39.810
They're actually, in several
reviews we find that there's

00:07:39.811 --> 00:07:42.220
a lot of apps that even
have maybe harmful content.

00:07:42.221 --> 00:07:45.533
There's one that's cited
pretty regularly for

00:07:45.534 --> 00:07:49.571
bipolar disorder where it tells
you if your moods are kind of

00:07:49.572 --> 00:07:51.740
getting more disruptive.

00:07:51.741 --> 00:07:54.350
This mood swings are very common
bipolar, you might try to

00:07:54.351 --> 00:07:58.040
help it by drinking more cuz
that will depress your moods.

00:07:58.041 --> 00:08:00.850
And it's,
this information is out there.

00:08:00.851 --> 00:08:05.132
And it's, if it's these apps
are sort of useless at best

00:08:05.133 --> 00:08:08.090
sometimes, and
harmful at the worst.

00:08:08.091 --> 00:08:12.590
And so, we really need
a lot more resources and

00:08:12.591 --> 00:08:15.980
efforts to try to be able to
provide useful information to

00:08:15.981 --> 00:08:19.880
consumers about what mental
health apps are out there.

00:08:19.881 --> 00:08:22.365
Which ones might be useful,
which ones might be good.

00:08:22.366 --> 00:08:26.940
That sort of typical way we
think about evaluating things in

00:08:26.941 --> 00:08:29.910
medicine and clinical psychology
is through sort of a gold

00:08:29.911 --> 00:08:31.340
standard randomized
control trial.

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:36.440
Where we have random assignment
to two different programs,

00:08:36.441 --> 00:08:39.600
you do a pre-test, you do
the program, you do a post test.

00:08:39.601 --> 00:08:40.590
This is really great.

00:08:40.591 --> 00:08:43.350
But unfortunately,
to do sort of the size of trials

00:08:43.351 --> 00:08:46.530
that we typically think about
in clinical psychology as what

00:08:46.531 --> 00:08:48.720
we would want in this gold
standard measurements.

00:08:48.721 --> 00:08:51.370
This is a extremely
slow process and

00:08:51.371 --> 00:08:54.620
so I'm not saying
that we shouldn't

00:08:54.621 --> 00:08:57.160
evaluate some things through
randomized control trials.

00:08:57.161 --> 00:09:00.470
But I also think that in
the context of digital mental

00:09:00.471 --> 00:09:04.930
health, we have to think about
other evaluation strategies.

00:09:04.931 --> 00:09:06.840
That can supplement
the information we gained

00:09:06.841 --> 00:09:08.135
from randomized control trials.

00:09:08.136 --> 00:09:10.099
So, as Mary mentioned,

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:15.570
I am the executive director of
a project called PsyberGuide.

00:09:15.571 --> 00:09:18.820
The goal at PsyberGuide is
to identify, curate, and

00:09:18.821 --> 00:09:22.600
disseminate information
about mental health apps.

00:09:22.601 --> 00:09:24.396
We're funded by
the One Mind Institute,

00:09:24.397 --> 00:09:27.850
which is a non-profit that
promotes mental health research.

00:09:27.851 --> 00:09:31.893
And we try to provide unbiased
information, kind of like

00:09:31.894 --> 00:09:35.358
the consumer reports of
digital mental health.

00:09:35.359 --> 00:09:38.010
So what does the PsyberGuide
website look like?

00:09:38.011 --> 00:09:40.272
What sort of things do
we provide for folks?

00:09:40.273 --> 00:09:42.630
We just try to organize
different mental health apps

00:09:42.631 --> 00:09:44.579
around different
disorder categories.

00:09:45.650 --> 00:09:48.963
Ranging from mood disorders,
things like depression,

00:09:48.964 --> 00:09:52.000
schizophrenia, serious
mental illness, PTSD and

00:09:52.001 --> 00:09:53.597
other anxiety disorders.

00:09:53.598 --> 00:09:58.212
And as well as sort of, general
wellness tools made to promote

00:09:58.213 --> 00:10:01.155
mental health sort
of more Generally.

00:10:01.156 --> 00:10:03.840
We divide things up also by the
type of strategy that they have.

00:10:03.841 --> 00:10:08.302
I'll say that most of the tools
in the area of depression and

00:10:08.303 --> 00:10:12.598
anxiety tend to be cognitive
behavioral therapy based.

00:10:12.599 --> 00:10:14.906
This is the strongest.

00:10:14.907 --> 00:10:18.467
Well, I should say, this is
the treatment with the strongest

00:10:18.468 --> 00:10:21.135
evidence based in
clinical psychology.

00:10:21.136 --> 00:10:24.005
Does not necessarily mean that
it's the strongest treatment.

00:10:24.006 --> 00:10:28.101
It means that it's the most
manualized treatment, so

00:10:28.102 --> 00:10:32.905
that I mean that we as clinical
psychologists kind of lay down.

00:10:32.906 --> 00:10:34.809
If you wanna do cognitive
behavioral therapy,

00:10:34.810 --> 00:10:35.670
this is how you do it.

00:10:35.671 --> 00:10:37.320
This is what your
sessions could cover.

00:10:37.321 --> 00:10:38.616
These are the tools
that you need.

00:10:38.617 --> 00:10:41.409
And so, it's been subjected to
the most of these randomized

00:10:41.410 --> 00:10:42.410
controlled trials.

00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:45.773
So I'm not saying,
it's the only thing that works.

00:10:45.774 --> 00:10:48.428
But I'm saying that it's
the thing with the most evidence

00:10:48.429 --> 00:10:49.630
that shows that it works.

00:10:50.710 --> 00:10:52.985
We also have a lot
of symptom trackers.

00:10:52.986 --> 00:10:55.300
These are very popular as
I'll talk about later.

00:10:55.301 --> 00:10:56.356
A lot of mindfulness tools.

00:10:56.357 --> 00:10:58.995
I'll say just as sort of
a point of what people

00:10:58.996 --> 00:11:01.806
are interested in
mobile mental health.

00:11:01.807 --> 00:11:03.908
On the PsyberGuide website
besides our main page and

00:11:03.909 --> 00:11:06.154
our product guide which
obviously have the most views,

00:11:06.155 --> 00:11:08.511
