00:11:51.551 --> 00:11:53.498
One is the PsyberGuide rating,

00:11:53.499 --> 00:11:56.050
which is a measure
of credibility.

00:11:56.051 --> 00:11:57.365
Through the PsyberGuide rating,

00:11:57.366 --> 00:11:58.822
we look at sort of
the evidence based.

00:11:58.823 --> 00:12:01.512
Is there scientific evidence
that suggests that this mental

00:12:01.513 --> 00:12:03.000
health app is useful?

00:12:03.001 --> 00:12:06.223
Is it created by a scientific
team or does it have scientist,

00:12:06.224 --> 00:12:09.322
or clinical advisor support
that would suggest that these

00:12:09.323 --> 00:12:11.215
are people who know
how to create and

00:12:11.216 --> 00:12:13.639
design effective
clinical interventions?

00:12:13.640 --> 00:12:15.520
Is it updated frequently?

00:12:15.521 --> 00:12:18.297
Does it have support behind?

00:12:18.298 --> 00:12:21.431
The product, is it coming
from a non profit or

00:12:21.432 --> 00:12:24.590
is it coming from a for
profit organization?

00:12:24.591 --> 00:12:27.837
But next thing we look at
is using something called

00:12:27.838 --> 00:12:29.781
the mobile app rating skill.

00:12:29.782 --> 00:12:32.389
This is a measure that was
developed from folks out of

00:12:32.390 --> 00:12:35.900
the University of Queensland
to evaluate help apps.

00:12:35.901 --> 00:12:38.524
It focuses a lot on like the
usability and user experience.

00:12:38.525 --> 00:12:41.244
So it could be that
there is a app that has

00:12:41.245 --> 00:12:44.880
very good credibility,
but it's very hard to use.

00:12:44.881 --> 00:12:46.529
I think we see this a lot.

00:12:46.530 --> 00:12:49.537
Because often times in
scientific studies to get people

00:12:49.538 --> 00:12:52.546
to use apps to to get people
to participate in the study,

00:12:52.547 --> 00:12:56.000
there are incentivized
through financial incentives.

00:12:56.001 --> 00:12:58.535
There might be a lot of sort of
education on how to use that app

00:12:58.536 --> 00:13:00.072
in that randomized
control trial.

00:13:00.073 --> 00:13:02.780
So that means if people are
really motivated to use this and

00:13:02.781 --> 00:13:05.211
they get a lot of sort of
troubleshooting around it,

00:13:05.212 --> 00:13:07.030
they'll use it and
they can benefit.

00:13:07.031 --> 00:13:09.780
But if you put it out there and
you don't have that support,

00:13:09.781 --> 00:13:11.600
people might have
a hard time using it.

00:13:11.601 --> 00:13:15.999
So I think that, again, you kind
of need both of these things to

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:18.490
make a really
useful application.

00:13:18.491 --> 00:13:22.717
I'm sure I'm preaching to the
choir here that effectiveness,

00:13:22.718 --> 00:13:26.642
usefulness is not just does it
work, but can people use it.

00:13:26.643 --> 00:13:29.323
But this is an important piece
that is usually left out in

00:13:29.324 --> 00:13:32.006
a lot of the psychological
discussion from the clinical

00:13:32.007 --> 00:13:34.390
psychology background
of these tools.

00:13:34.391 --> 00:13:36.448
And then lastly,
we have expert reviews for

00:13:36.449 --> 00:13:39.226
some of these apps which is we
have an expert in the field that

00:13:39.227 --> 00:13:40.465
knows that content area.

00:13:40.466 --> 00:13:42.475
That's cognitive
behavioral therapy app.

00:13:42.476 --> 00:13:44.517
For depression, you might
have a depression expert.

00:13:44.518 --> 00:13:45.515
It's a mindfulness app.

00:13:45.516 --> 00:13:48.715
We might have a mindfulness
expert, but they create more

00:13:48.716 --> 00:13:51.780
a narrative for users about
how you might use this app,

00:13:51.781 --> 00:13:53.793
how it might be able
to benefit you.

00:13:53.794 --> 00:13:57.820
So, just a little bit of
discussion around what we found

00:13:57.821 --> 00:14:02.399
from mental health apps through
this endeavor of rating them.

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:06.786
From PsyberGuide rating, we rate
everything on a 14-point scale

00:14:06.787 --> 00:14:09.746
and then present everything
as a percentage.

00:14:09.747 --> 00:14:14.520
So apps can range from 0 to 100,
hypothetically.

00:14:14.521 --> 00:14:18.842
Our actual range is 14 through
93% with an average of 52%.

00:14:18.843 --> 00:14:24.130
So, there's some apps that look
really credible out there.

00:14:24.131 --> 00:14:25.826
There's some apps
that look really bad.

00:14:25.827 --> 00:14:29.510
It's PsyberGuide, we try to
not just rate the good apps.

00:14:29.511 --> 00:14:35.929
Because we feel if someone finds
an app, we want them to know.

00:14:35.930 --> 00:14:38.886
If they find brain each queue,

00:14:38.887 --> 00:14:43.220
we want them to know like,
is this a good app?

00:14:43.221 --> 00:14:43.854
Is this not a good app?

00:14:43.855 --> 00:14:47.909
And so we really try to shy away
from the strategy of identifying

00:14:47.910 --> 00:14:50.140
and recommending specific apps.

00:14:50.141 --> 00:14:53.415
Our goal is really to put
the information out there in

00:14:53.416 --> 00:14:55.431
a useful way for our consumers.

00:14:55.432 --> 00:14:59.546
So you can see from the
percentiles that It does look

00:14:59.547 --> 00:15:04.720
like a lot of the apps do pretty
well on this credibility rating.

00:15:04.721 --> 00:15:06.875
Well, a lot of apps,
let's just say,

00:15:06.876 --> 00:15:09.160
cluster around the center in
terms of credibility rating.

00:15:09.161 --> 00:15:11.087
So, there's not a lot
at this high end,

00:15:11.088 --> 00:15:12.748
there's not a lot
at this low end.

00:15:12.749 --> 00:15:14.802
There's a lot of apps
that there's some,

00:15:14.803 --> 00:15:16.920
it might be useful,
we don't really know.

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:21.100
For the MARS ratings,

00:15:21.101 --> 00:15:23.500
60% of our products have
these MARS ratings.

00:15:23.501 --> 00:15:26.530
Every product listed has
a PsyberGuide rating.

00:15:26.531 --> 00:15:32.530
The range here again is 1.63 to
4.75 with an average of 3.5,

00:15:32.531 --> 00:15:36.470
so things do okay on
this user rating scale.

00:15:36.471 --> 00:15:39.720
Again, the interquartile range
shows that a lot of stuff

00:15:39.721 --> 00:15:41.760
kind of clusters in
the middle there.

00:15:41.761 --> 00:15:44.378
I'll say that these
credibility and

00:15:44.379 --> 00:15:48.610
these MARS rating scales are
almost not correlated at all.

00:15:48.611 --> 00:15:52.200
So the correlation between these
things are about an R of 0.2.

00:15:52.201 --> 00:15:56.158
And I say not correlated at all
because practically anything you

00:15:56.159 --> 00:16:00.270
correlate in psychology will get
an R of 0.2, it just happens.

00:16:00.271 --> 00:16:01.860
Things are somewhat correlated.

00:16:01.861 --> 00:16:05.340
They also don't correlate
very highly with our

00:16:05.341 --> 00:16:08.040
ratings off of
the public app stores.

00:16:08.041 --> 00:16:11.410
Neither the Mars rating nor
the PsyberGuide rating.

00:16:11.411 --> 00:16:15.410
So it seems like if we just
look at the user ratings and

00:16:15.411 --> 00:16:18.090
scores, but that's not
really representing either

00:16:18.091 --> 00:16:20.810
the credibility or
the user experience of that.

00:16:20.811 --> 00:16:23.480
So it's hard to say then from a
user's perspective when they go

00:16:23.481 --> 00:16:26.020
and rate this applications
on app stores

00:16:26.021 --> 00:16:27.170
what they're
actually looking at.

00:16:27.171 --> 00:16:30.230
I'm also saying in terms of
public facing app store ratings,

00:16:30.231 --> 00:16:31.940
so thanks off to
Google Play Store and

00:16:31.941 --> 00:16:32.859
the Apple iTunes Store.

00:16:34.120 --> 00:16:35.670
There's a lot of
these applications

00:16:35.671 --> 00:16:38.550
that are very popular in either
the clinical literature or

00:16:38.551 --> 00:16:45.351
the popular literature that
have no public sort of

00:16:45.352 --> 00:16:49.320
rating on the app stores because
they don't have enough ratings.

00:16:49.321 --> 00:16:52.470
That at least on the iTunes
Store, you need a certain number

00:16:52.471 --> 00:16:54.730
of ratings before they
actually display the average.

00:16:54.731 --> 00:16:58.070
And usually most of these mental
health apps don't have them.

00:16:58.071 --> 00:17:01.825
I think about 10% of the apps
that we've looked at

00:17:01.826 --> 00:17:05.545
actually have publicly available
ratings on the app stores.

00:17:07.185 --> 00:17:09.425
In terms of the expert reviews,

00:17:09.426 --> 00:17:11.765
about 30% of our products
have expert reviews.

00:17:13.085 --> 00:17:14.505
And so

00:17:14.506 --> 00:17:17.345
the expert reviews do look at
the higher range of the product.

00:17:17.346 --> 00:17:20.233
So we have a average
Psyberguide rating of 60 and

00:17:20.234 --> 00:17:22.390
an average MARS rating of 3.79.

00:17:22.391 --> 00:17:25.380
So again, we try to focus on the
upper range of products there.

00:17:25.381 --> 00:17:27.810
We don't really feel
like we need to provide

00:17:27.811 --> 00:17:30.610
consumers with a lot of
information about how to use

00:17:30.611 --> 00:17:33.330
the products that we don't
seem to be all that good.

00:17:33.331 --> 00:17:35.470
We really wanna direct people
to things that we think

00:17:35.471 --> 00:17:36.220
might be more useful.

00:17:37.840 --> 00:17:39.800
So one of the things actually
we'd like to do more in

00:17:39.801 --> 00:17:42.140
the future with PsyberGuide
is we wanna look more

00:17:42.141 --> 00:17:45.420
also at integrating
the user experience of

00:17:45.421 --> 00:17:47.320
their impression of
these applications.

00:17:47.321 --> 00:17:51.475
As I've said,
the public consumer

00:17:51.476 --> 00:17:54.480
marketplaces of app stores is
that those ratings don't really

00:17:54.481 --> 00:17:56.630
relate to the information
that we're gathering.

00:17:56.631 --> 00:17:59.470
And so we like to gain more
understanding of what it is that

00:17:59.471 --> 00:18:01.420
people like and don't like
about applications and

00:18:01.421 --> 00:18:03.250
how we can present
that back to them.

00:18:03.251 --> 00:18:05.610
So that's one thing that we're
really kind of focusing on now

00:18:05.611 --> 00:18:10.445
is how we engage the consumers
as stakeholders in terms

00:18:10.446 --> 00:18:13.335
of contributing information
that we can then disseminate.

00:18:13.336 --> 00:18:16.152
I think this is a really
big need for mental,

00:18:16.153 --> 00:18:19.352
digital mental health more
generally is engaging

00:18:19.353 --> 00:18:23.236
stakeholders more in the process
of both the development and

00:18:23.237 --> 00:18:25.232
the evaluation piece of this.

00:18:27.839 --> 00:18:28.339
Yeah.

00:18:29.340 --> 00:18:32.499
&gt;&gt; Does the FDA regulate any
of these mental health apps?

00:18:32.500 --> 00:18:33.016
&gt;&gt; The FDA does

00:18:33.017 --> 00:18:35.510
not currently regulate any
of these mental health apps.

00:18:35.511 --> 00:18:40.135
The FDA only regulates health
apps if they are used for

00:18:40.136 --> 00:18:43.365
the purposes of assessment,
of providing an intervention.

00:18:43.366 --> 00:18:46.885
So, if you were,
if you developed an app that

00:18:46.886 --> 00:18:49.305
was meant to sort of diagnose
you for a mental disorder and

00:18:49.306 --> 00:18:51.595
then provide that
information into clinician.

00:18:51.596 --> 00:18:54.115
It would be arguable
that that would be,

00:18:54.116 --> 00:18:56.840
should be regulated by the FDA.

00:18:56.841 --> 00:18:59.210
These apps are really sold
as sort of do it yourself or

00:18:59.211 --> 00:19:00.580
self management tools and so

00:19:00.581 --> 00:19:02.530
they don't fall under
the FDA regulation.

00:19:02.531 --> 00:19:08.890
There is one app I know that was
on track for FDA approval and

00:19:08.891 --> 00:19:11.800
that was an app that was
created by Para Therapeutics

00:19:11.801 --> 00:19:15.090
that was made to be used in
the context of substance abuse.

00:19:15.091 --> 00:19:18.340
But that was one again,
that helps diagnose people and

00:19:18.341 --> 00:19:20.570
then provided the information
to their provider.

00:19:20.571 --> 00:19:23.640
So it really has to be
under the current sort of

00:19:23.641 --> 00:19:27.120
FDA purview of what they've
said they're gonna regulate in

00:19:27.121 --> 00:19:28.460
digital health space.

00:19:28.461 --> 00:19:32.070
It needs to be used in
the context of treatment.

00:19:32.071 --> 00:19:34.573
&gt;&gt; So mental health is similar
then to the fitness apps that

00:19:34.574 --> 00:19:35.753
are out there [INAUDIBLE]?

00:19:35.754 --> 00:19:36.851
&gt;&gt; Exactly, yep, yep.

00:19:36.852 --> 00:19:38.210
&gt;&gt; Thanks.

00:19:38.211 --> 00:19:41.860
&gt;&gt; Is there anyway of
recording appers events?

00:19:41.861 --> 00:19:44.235
So does anybody track these?

00:19:44.236 --> 00:19:46.878
&gt;&gt; No,
not in sort of the public space.

00:19:46.879 --> 00:19:50.568
I think and that's one thing I
think is also really important

00:19:50.569 --> 00:19:54.039
is where, this bipolar app I
mentioned that has this very

00:19:54.040 --> 00:19:57.602
sort of Potentially dangerous
recommendation in there.

00:19:57.603 --> 00:20:01.020
And I think, again, right now
it's a very sort of wild lust.

00:20:01.021 --> 00:20:03.100
And that would be a good
thing to kind of get.

00:20:03.101 --> 00:20:06.028
It's like collecting more
information about when these

00:20:06.029 --> 00:20:07.498
apps do go seriously wrong.

00:20:07.499 --> 00:20:09.027
&gt;&gt; And
I apologize if I'm mistaken.

00:20:09.028 --> 00:20:13.770
I believe that APA also
has an app ratings system.

00:20:13.771 --> 00:20:15.750
&gt;&gt; By APA do you mean
the American Psychological

00:20:15.751 --> 00:20:18.130
Association or the American
Psychiatric Association?

00:20:18.131 --> 00:20:18.905
&gt;&gt; Shoot, I don't know.

00:20:18.906 --> 00:20:19.494
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:20:19.495 --> 00:20:23.228
the American Psychiatric
Association does have an app

00:20:23.229 --> 00:20:24.460
rating system.

00:20:24.461 --> 00:20:26.610
It follows a lot of the same
kind of guidelines of what we

00:20:26.611 --> 00:20:28.140
do in PsyberGuide.

00:20:28.141 --> 00:20:31.200
It's meant more to be
a consumer framework.

00:20:31.201 --> 00:20:35.270
So what the American Psychiatric
Association has done is it

00:20:35.271 --> 00:20:38.190
sets up sort of a tiered system
of if you're a consumer and

00:20:38.191 --> 00:20:40.050
you wanna look at
a mental health app,

00:20:40.051 --> 00:20:42.590
these are the things
you should consider.

00:20:42.591 --> 00:20:46.355
Privacy and security,
effectiveness, user experience,

00:20:46.356 --> 00:20:47.841
interoperatibility.

00:20:47.842 --> 00:20:51.389
I think that's really great but
I think as a consumer,

00:20:51.390 --> 00:20:55.100
I would have, if you told me
about if I want to go buy a car.

00:20:55.101 --> 00:20:57.420
And you told me these are the
things that you should consider

00:20:57.421 --> 00:20:58.710
when you're buying a car.

00:20:58.711 --> 00:21:02.070
Safety, gas mileage.

00:21:02.071 --> 00:21:04.890
All these things, you can tell
me the value of those things but

00:21:04.891 --> 00:21:06.000
I'm not gonna know how to do it.

00:21:06.001 --> 00:21:09.227
And so I think that's a little
bit of where I think that

00:21:09.228 --> 00:21:12.530
the American Psychiatric
Association framework does

00:21:12.531 --> 00:21:15.560
a really nice job of saying
what we should look at.

00:21:15.561 --> 00:21:18.345
But I think that consumers often
need that sort of extra step to

00:21:18.346 --> 00:21:18.889
get there.

00:21:18.890 --> 00:21:21.390
&gt;&gt; Definitely, did you use
it as kind of a guide?

00:21:21.391 --> 00:21:24.220
&gt;&gt; A lot of the sort of areas
that we cover overlap with

00:21:24.221 --> 00:21:27.470
the American Psychiatric
Association with the exception

00:21:27.471 --> 00:21:29.700
of the privacy security section.

00:21:29.701 --> 00:21:32.350
That's something we're
debating a lot internally.

00:21:32.351 --> 00:21:36.467
So there was a group that tried
to evaluate health apps more

00:21:36.468 --> 00:21:39.250
generally, known as Haptic.

00:21:39.251 --> 00:21:43.330
And Haptic got in big trouble
because they had a model where

00:21:43.331 --> 00:21:47.628
they would do individual sort
of certification of apps.

00:21:47.629 --> 00:21:50.920
Kind of like you would want
the FDA to do perhaps.

00:21:50.921 --> 00:21:53.538
And they got into hot water when
there are a couple apps that

00:21:53.539 --> 00:21:54.773
they sort of signed off on.

00:21:54.774 --> 00:21:57.787
It was shown that they were
actually selling users' data to

00:21:57.788 --> 00:21:58.760
third parties.

00:21:58.761 --> 00:22:03.130
And so I think that's one of the
problems that you run into with

00:22:03.131 --> 00:22:07.450
this privacy and security
is that, it's hard to know

00:22:07.451 --> 00:22:10.800
sometimes with these apps where
the data actually is going,

00:22:10.801 --> 00:22:12.090
what people are doing with data.

00:22:12.091 --> 00:22:15.030
And some organizations
are not so clear with that.

00:22:15.031 --> 00:22:18.710
And so again,
it's hard to provide sort

00:22:18.711 --> 00:22:21.290
of recommendation of, this
is a secure app if you don't

00:22:21.291 --> 00:22:22.760
have of all of the information
from it yourself.

00:22:24.180 --> 00:22:26.910
&gt;&gt; If I'm in the App Store and
I'm looking for

00:22:26.911 --> 00:22:32.140
an anxiety app, how would I even
know that PsyberGuide existed?

00:22:32.141 --> 00:22:32.950
&gt;&gt; That's a great question.

00:22:32.951 --> 00:22:35.860
I think that that's one
thing that we are trying

00:22:35.861 --> 00:22:38.030
to do is partner
with organizations.

00:22:38.031 --> 00:22:40.910
We have an agreement with Mental
Health America to partner with

00:22:40.911 --> 00:22:44.340
their screening exercise.

00:22:44.341 --> 00:22:47.820
Where people get screened
through Mental Health America,

00:22:47.821 --> 00:22:50.000
and then can get recommendations
for different services.

00:22:52.620 --> 00:22:55.419
Right now there's no way of
knowing about it, and I think

00:22:55.420 --> 00:22:58.688
without teaming with those app
stores themselves, that's gonna

00:22:58.689 --> 00:23:01.327
be a problem that's not gonna
be able to be overcome.

00:23:01.328 --> 00:23:04.469
So I wanna transition to a
little bit more research around

00:23:04.470 --> 00:23:07.610
how and why people use
mental health apps.

00:23:07.611 --> 00:23:10.440
This is a penny arcade comic
that I really like that makes me

00:23:10.441 --> 00:23:11.890
think about this from years ago.

00:23:11.891 --> 00:23:13.840
Where apps were really
just blowing up and

00:23:13.841 --> 00:23:16.490
the two characters are fighting
about whether one should buy

00:23:16.491 --> 00:23:17.860
a console game or app games.

00:23:17.861 --> 00:23:19.860
And I love this quote here,

00:23:19.861 --> 00:23:22.760
well you buy one 3DS
Nintendo game and

00:23:22.761 --> 00:23:25.860
I'll buy 40 games on my phone
and we'll see who has more fun.

00:23:25.861 --> 00:23:28.700
So sort of this idea of yeah,
you can support using $40 on one

00:23:28.701 --> 00:23:31.000
game or I can spend $40
on 40 different games and

00:23:31.001 --> 00:23:32.880
we'll see who actually gets
more enjoyment out of this.

00:23:32.881 --> 00:23:33.590
Kind of arguing for

00:23:33.591 --> 00:23:37.184
this model of diversity
in terms of products.

00:23:38.350 --> 00:23:41.350
So one of the things
that we did recently

00:23:41.351 --> 00:23:45.990
is we looked at users who were
interested in entering into

00:23:45.991 --> 00:23:48.730
different clinical trials that
we were doing at the Center for

00:23:48.731 --> 00:23:50.800
Behavioral and
Intervention Technologies.

00:23:50.801 --> 00:23:53.240
And before they got onboarded
into any of our trials,

00:23:53.241 --> 00:23:56.370
we actually just
simply asked them what

00:23:56.371 --> 00:23:58.150
sort of health apps do
you have on your phone,

00:23:58.151 --> 00:24:00.780
what sort of mental health apps
do you have on your phone?

00:24:00.781 --> 00:24:02.150
Why do you use these apps?

00:24:02.151 --> 00:24:03.660
What are the app names?

00:24:03.661 --> 00:24:07.730
And we found that most
people use multiple apps.

00:24:07.731 --> 00:24:12.400
The average was two,
there's a range from 0 to 20.

00:24:12.401 --> 00:24:15.197
So again, this isn't suggesting
that people are finding one

00:24:15.198 --> 00:24:16.773
health app and they're using it.

00:24:16.774 --> 00:24:20.401
They're using this penny arcade
sort of model of trying a bunch

00:24:20.402 --> 00:24:23.962
of different things and
seeing what's helpful for them.

00:24:23.963 --> 00:24:26.370
About one-fourth of them
were mental health or

00:24:26.371 --> 00:24:29.330
wellness apps, which corresponds

00:24:30.870 --> 00:24:33.950
somewhat with the sort of
overall representation.

00:24:33.951 --> 00:24:37.578
Again, probably this number is
a bit elevated from the general

00:24:37.579 --> 00:24:41.137
population because these are all
people who are entering into

00:24:41.138 --> 00:24:44.161
a clinical trial that was
focused on mental health.

00:24:44.162 --> 00:24:48.033
92.8% were free.

00:24:48.034 --> 00:24:51.613
And it's worth knowing that
this is not representative of

00:24:51.614 --> 00:24:55.633
the overall sort of marketplace
of mental health apps, so People

00:24:55.634 --> 00:24:59.434
are disproportionately focused
on downloading free apps for

00:24:59.435 --> 00:25:02.740
their health, which I think
is really interesting.

00:25:02.741 --> 00:25:05.600
And also, again, it's not
representative if you just look

00:25:05.601 --> 00:25:10.450
at the marketplace review
of what's out there.

00:25:11.840 --> 00:25:14.320
In our data, we found that if
we looked at the purposes for

00:25:14.321 --> 00:25:17.140
the applications, the most
common reason people said

00:25:17.141 --> 00:25:19.580
they were using mental
health applications was for

00:25:19.581 --> 00:25:20.660
tracking purposes.

00:25:20.661 --> 00:25:23.870
About 40% of the apps
that people were using,

00:25:23.871 --> 00:25:28.720
they said they were using for
some sort of tracking purpose.

00:25:28.721 --> 00:25:30.910
What was really interesting to
me actually was that when we

00:25:30.911 --> 00:25:33.020
looked at the names of
the different apps that people

00:25:33.021 --> 00:25:37.070
were using, there was
not a large proportion.

00:25:37.071 --> 00:25:40.993
But there was a proportion of
apps that they had noted that

00:25:40.994 --> 00:25:43.042
were not mental health apps.

00:25:43.043 --> 00:25:45.000
They were not
health apps at all.

00:25:45.001 --> 00:25:46.590
They were things like Snapchat,

00:25:46.591 --> 00:25:49.860
like Google Documents,
like Facebook, like Twitter.

00:25:49.861 --> 00:25:52.870
And people actually reported
using these different things for

00:25:52.871 --> 00:25:54.270
health purposes.

00:25:54.271 --> 00:25:57.791
And so I think that's another
interesting piece to this puzzle

00:25:57.792 --> 00:25:59.985
here that as clinical
psychologists,

00:25:59.986 --> 00:26:01.718
we often look at the app store.

00:26:01.719 --> 00:26:02.542
We look at things.

00:26:02.543 --> 00:26:03.716
People search for anxiety.

00:26:03.717 --> 00:26:04.705
People search for depression.

00:26:04.706 --> 00:26:06.632
And those are the things
we're evaluating.

00:26:06.633 --> 00:26:10.044
But there's a lot of way
that people might be using

00:26:10.045 --> 00:26:12.815
non-health apps for
health purposes.

00:26:12.816 --> 00:26:16.036
And I think that we as a field
should better understand when

00:26:16.037 --> 00:26:17.095
that's going on.

00:26:17.096 --> 00:26:18.355
What people are getting
out of that.

00:26:18.356 --> 00:26:20.918
And how we can actually maybe
build some of these tools to

00:26:20.919 --> 00:26:23.588
better meet the mental health
needs that people are having

00:26:23.589 --> 00:26:26.117
when they're going through
these different things.

00:26:28.821 --> 00:26:31.264
So I wanna loop back
to that IMS report.

00:26:31.265 --> 00:26:34.874
Because I think it actually has
some interesting points to make

00:26:34.875 --> 00:26:38.290
about what the apps actually
do in terms of functionality.

00:26:39.810 --> 00:26:43.207
So back in 2015 when the IMS
looked at all of the different

00:26:43.208 --> 00:26:45.072
health apps that are available,

00:26:45.073 --> 00:26:48.814
they noticed that about half the
apps had a single functionality.

00:26:48.815 --> 00:26:51.790
So that means that most
of the apps do one thing.

00:26:51.791 --> 00:26:53.110
And that most of the time,

00:26:53.111 --> 00:26:55.220
that functionality
was to inform people.

00:26:55.221 --> 00:26:58.137
So effectively, most health
apps that are out there

00:26:58.138 --> 00:27:01.333
are psychoeducational tools
that teach people things.

00:27:01.334 --> 00:27:05.993
They're self-help books that had
been made into an app format.

00:27:05.994 --> 00:27:06.992
Self-help books are great.

00:27:06.993 --> 00:27:08.040
We've had them for years.

00:27:08.041 --> 00:27:09.011
They can be useful for people.

00:27:09.012 --> 00:27:12.909
But I think what's going on
here is a sad opportunity,

00:27:12.910 --> 00:27:15.451
a lost opportunity,
I should say,

00:27:15.452 --> 00:27:19.521
to use the true affordances
of digital technologies to do

00:27:19.522 --> 00:27:22.780
really creative things
in the health space.

00:27:24.390 --> 00:27:29.340
There's, I think it was about 5%
here of apps that were used for

00:27:29.341 --> 00:27:31.417
communication purposes.

00:27:31.418 --> 00:27:34.332
So the ability to connect to
people, either to each other.

00:27:34.333 --> 00:27:36.930
And that was actually what
was most common is that.

00:27:36.931 --> 00:27:39.990
So of that small percentage of
apps that have communication

00:27:39.991 --> 00:27:41.720
purposes, most of the time,

00:27:41.721 --> 00:27:43.970
those apps are allowing people
to connect to social media.

00:27:45.060 --> 00:27:46.870
Maybe that's the best thing for
health apps to do,

00:27:46.871 --> 00:27:47.650
maybe it's not.

00:27:47.651 --> 00:27:49.050
I'm not gonna open
up the debate here.

00:27:49.051 --> 00:27:51.122
But I'm just saying
that's the most common

00:27:51.123 --> 00:27:53.475
communication purpose is
let's share our health

00:27:53.476 --> 00:27:54.970
information to social media.

00:27:54.971 --> 00:27:58.150
34 were just social networks,

00:27:58.151 --> 00:28:00.145
again, a social
component to that.

00:28:00.146 --> 00:28:02.370
2% went to providers.

00:28:02.371 --> 00:28:06.200
So very few health apps actually
have any sort of information of

00:28:06.201 --> 00:28:09.340
interoperability that allows
information to be translated

00:28:09.341 --> 00:28:11.040
from a user to a provider.

00:28:11.041 --> 00:28:13.860
Again, I think this is
a big missed opportunity.

00:28:13.861 --> 00:28:16.750
I was talking with Mary a little
bit about this earlier,

00:28:16.751 --> 00:28:20.080
about how we actually can take
information from different

00:28:20.081 --> 00:28:22.500
health apps and be able to
share that with providers.

00:28:22.501 --> 00:28:25.161
And I think we need a lot more
understanding of what providers

00:28:25.162 --> 00:28:26.525
want in those circumstances.

00:28:26.526 --> 00:28:29.201
What patients want in
those circumstances.

00:28:29.202 --> 00:28:32.863
And how to best translate
patient needs and

00:28:32.864 --> 00:28:35.930
wants to provider needs and
wants.

00:28:35.931 --> 00:28:38.310
So I wanna just, go ahead.

00:28:38.311 --> 00:28:40.683
&gt;&gt; Just curiously,
that 2% provider communication,

00:28:40.684 --> 00:28:43.210
is that related to just not
right building a functionality

00:28:43.211 --> 00:28:45.391
into the app to begin with
cuz they don't want to?

00:28:45.392 --> 00:28:49.420
Or is it because of EHR barrier,
integration barriers?

00:28:49.421 --> 00:28:50.440
&gt;&gt; That's a good question.

00:28:50.441 --> 00:28:52.430
I think it's probably
a little bit of both.

00:28:52.431 --> 00:28:56.430
I mean, getting information to
the EHR is a huge headache.

00:28:56.431 --> 00:28:57.637
I know at Northwestern,

00:28:57.638 --> 00:29:00.511
we're just starting this
new initiative called, man,

00:29:00.512 --> 00:29:02.815
it's like project one or
something like that.

00:29:02.816 --> 00:29:05.442
Where they're trying to bring
all of the instances of epic to

00:29:05.443 --> 00:29:08.800
have one version, where they'll
all communicate with each other.

00:29:08.801 --> 00:29:12.218
And in the process of doing
that, they've completely locked

00:29:12.219 --> 00:29:14.990
down introduction of new
information to the EHR.

00:29:14.991 --> 00:29:16.520
So we can't put any information.

00:29:16.521 --> 00:29:19.580
It's just whenever I do
projects, that is a non-starter.

00:29:19.581 --> 00:29:21.190
No information gets
to go into the EHR.

00:29:21.191 --> 00:29:23.389
I've seen some workarounds,

00:29:23.390 --> 00:29:26.420
where information can be
translated into a document that

00:29:26.421 --> 00:29:30.340
can be uploaded as a attachment
to a provider message.

00:29:30.341 --> 00:29:32.800
But direct integration
of the EHR

00:29:32.801 --> 00:29:34.710
has been a major headache for
any project I've worked on.

00:29:34.711 --> 00:29:37.535
So imagine that's a big barrier
for a lot of other people, too.

00:29:37.536 --> 00:29:38.604
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] So

00:29:38.605 --> 00:29:42.888
I don't know if you've
asked this question or not.

00:29:42.889 --> 00:29:47.260
Did you ask or find a new thing
in the literature about how

00:29:47.261 --> 00:29:51.363
people find out about
the health apps that they use?

00:29:51.364 --> 00:29:54.337
&gt;&gt; We didn't look in
that specific study.

00:29:54.338 --> 00:29:57.357
We have a current study where
we're trying to ask that

00:29:57.358 --> 00:29:58.966
question to find more like,

00:29:58.967 --> 00:30:01.005
where do you come
to health apps?

00:30:01.006 --> 00:30:04.160
And also what we're really
interested in the current state

00:30:04.161 --> 00:30:08.040
we're doing is when they look
for them, what information

00:30:08.041 --> 00:30:10.520
would be most valuable to them
to direct their decision?

00:30:10.521 --> 00:30:11.876
I think it's a big
open question.

00:30:11.877 --> 00:30:14.136
Yeah, I think it's
worth looking into.

00:30:14.137 --> 00:30:16.368
&gt;&gt; On the 2% of [INAUDIBLE]
communicate to providers,

00:30:16.369 --> 00:30:19.540
could providers actually do
anything with the information?

00:30:19.541 --> 00:30:21.930
Or is it still like you said
that's where you have to look at

00:30:24.150 --> 00:30:24.660
both sides?

00:30:24.661 --> 00:30:26.420
&gt;&gt; That's a tricky question.

00:30:26.421 --> 00:30:28.594
Can I come back to that
at the end of the talk?

00:30:28.595 --> 00:30:30.724
Cuz I think there might be
a little bit more discussion to

00:30:30.725 --> 00:30:31.457
have around that.

00:30:31.458 --> 00:30:33.310
But that's a really good and
tricky question.

00:30:34.380 --> 00:30:36.670
So I wanna actually go into
another platform that we've

00:30:36.671 --> 00:30:39.970
developed at Northwestern
that we call IntelliCare.

00:30:39.971 --> 00:30:43.383
And it takes a slightly
different design perspective to

00:30:43.384 --> 00:30:44.890
mobile mental health.

00:30:44.891 --> 00:30:48.520
So what IntelliCare is is
instead of a single app,

00:30:48.521 --> 00:30:51.650
we've developed
an app suite that's

00:30:51.651 --> 00:30:54.610
organized by this
hub application.

00:30:54.611 --> 00:30:57.660
So the IntelliCare app suite
actually has 12 different

00:30:57.661 --> 00:31:01.020
mini applications that
each focus on a specific

00:31:01.021 --> 00:31:02.310
behavior change technique.

00:31:03.610 --> 00:31:04.570
So here's a couple of them.

00:31:04.571 --> 00:31:06.035
This is actually one of
our most popular apps.

00:31:06.036 --> 00:31:07.440
It's called Daily Feats.

00:31:07.441 --> 00:31:09.840
When you sign up for the app,
you take a brief depression and

00:31:09.841 --> 00:31:10.660
anxiety screener.

00:31:10.661 --> 00:31:14.370
I'll say IntelliCare was
intentionally designed to deal

00:31:14.371 --> 00:31:16.930
with issues of depression and
anxiety.

00:31:16.931 --> 00:31:18.933
So you take a depression and
anxiety screener.

00:31:18.934 --> 00:31:21.820
It gives you a set of
tasks to do each day.

00:31:21.821 --> 00:31:24.910
Daily feats that are organized
around your level of

00:31:24.911 --> 00:31:26.350
depression and anxiety.

00:31:26.351 --> 00:31:29.310
So if you are pretty
depressed when you start out,

00:31:29.311 --> 00:31:30.790
you'll have just
very basic feats.

00:31:30.791 --> 00:31:33.620
Get out bed, groom yourself,
make good food choices,

00:31:33.621 --> 00:31:34.740
go outside.

00:31:34.741 --> 00:31:36.724
I will say these might
seem very basic to you.

00:31:36.725 --> 00:31:40.225
But the feedback we've gotten
from our users is that it's

00:31:40.226 --> 00:31:44.227
extremely empowering for them
when they're seriously depressed

00:31:44.228 --> 00:31:47.943
for us to reframe these things
that they truly struggle with as

00:31:47.944 --> 00:31:50.380
feats to be accomplished.

00:31:50.381 --> 00:31:51.514
So you open up the application.

00:31:51.515 --> 00:31:53.826
You check off which
things you do that day.

00:31:53.827 --> 00:31:57.351
If you form a streak
over multiple days,

00:31:57.352 --> 00:32:02.131
you get a little indication
in terms of green numbers.

00:32:02.132 --> 00:32:04.643
You have enough streaks
over subsequent days,

00:32:04.644 --> 00:32:06.475
you move up to
subsequent levels.

00:32:06.476 --> 00:32:08.684
And you continue to keep
leveling up, get more and

00:32:08.685 --> 00:32:10.220
more challenging things.

00:32:10.221 --> 00:32:12.590
Very simple application,
very easy to use, very,

00:32:12.591 --> 00:32:14.521
very popular in our suite.

00:32:14.522 --> 00:32:16.330
&gt;&gt; Define a streak.

00:32:16.331 --> 00:32:20.140
&gt;&gt; Five days of
completing a goal.

00:32:20.141 --> 00:32:21.610
This is an app that I developed.

00:32:21.611 --> 00:32:22.770
It's called MyMantra.

00:32:22.771 --> 00:32:25.810
I call it the Pinterest of
cognitive behavioral therapy.

00:32:25.811 --> 00:32:28.936
You collect pictures that
organize around a positive

00:32:28.937 --> 00:32:31.120
self-affirming mantra.

00:32:31.121 --> 00:32:34.840
There's a couple example ones
in here in the application.

00:32:34.841 --> 00:32:39.761
Things like I'm strong,
I'm grateful, I'm resilient.

00:32:39.762 --> 00:32:41.637
And as you collect these photos,

00:32:41.638 --> 00:32:44.588
you get them fed back to
you through notifications.

00:32:44.589 --> 00:32:47.830
Again, very, very simple,
a popular app within our suite.

00:32:48.890 --> 00:32:49.730
This is Thought Challenger.

00:32:49.731 --> 00:32:53.355
It's maybe the most classic
mental health app that's in

00:32:53.356 --> 00:32:54.950
the IntelliCare suite.

00:32:56.030 --> 00:32:57.911
It helps walk you through
the process of cognitive

00:32:57.912 --> 00:32:58.592
restructuring.

00:32:58.593 --> 00:33:02.890
Which is this idea that the
content of your thoughts is more

00:33:02.891 --> 00:33:05.795
important than
the actual situation.

00:33:05.796 --> 00:33:08.441
It helps you come up with
more positive ways to view

00:33:08.442 --> 00:33:09.200
situations.

00:33:10.620 --> 00:33:14.050
So you come up with a negative,
unrealistic thought.

00:33:14.051 --> 00:33:16.420
You substitute it with a more
realistic way of thinking.

00:33:16.421 --> 00:33:19.330
This walks you through the
five-step process of doing that.

00:33:19.331 --> 00:33:20.220
And on the home screen,

00:33:20.221 --> 00:33:23.700
you have all of the good,
positive reframe thoughts,

00:33:23.701 --> 00:33:25.760
a word cloud that's
generated from that content.

00:33:25.761 --> 00:33:27.540
We wanna throw the positive
content back at

00:33:27.541 --> 00:33:29.286
people as opposed to
the negative content.

00:33:29.287 --> 00:33:36.740
So we did a 8-week field trial
of the IntelliCare platform.

00:33:36.741 --> 00:33:39.581
I will say that it was done
along with coached support.

00:33:39.582 --> 00:33:43.122
We had a person providing some
light encouragement engagement

00:33:43.123 --> 00:33:45.320
through text messaging.

00:33:45.321 --> 00:33:47.566
This is the result from that
single arm field trial.

00:33:47.567 --> 00:33:51.078
We found that people over 8
weeks experienced significant

00:33:51.079 --> 00:33:55.130
reductions in their levels of
depression as well as anxiety.

00:33:55.131 --> 00:33:58.572
We just recently wrapped
up a 300-person,

00:33:58.573 --> 00:34:02.796
4-armed randomized controlled
trial of intelliCare.

00:34:02.797 --> 00:34:04.430
So we'll have more
data on this soon.

00:34:06.780 --> 00:34:11.628
This again shows similar thing
but looks at the percent of

00:34:11.629 --> 00:34:15.567
people who are in full
remission or recovery.

00:34:15.568 --> 00:34:20.260
Which is indicated by clinically
set cut points in terms of

00:34:20.261 --> 00:34:23.050
reductions on their same skills.

00:34:23.051 --> 00:34:26.104
So people reduced in their
depression and anxiety.

00:34:26.105 --> 00:34:30.525
This was actually one of the
more exciting findings to me,

00:34:30.526 --> 00:34:34.045
which is that the use of
IntelliCare was pretty

00:34:34.046 --> 00:34:37.140
consistent over
that 8-week period.

00:34:37.141 --> 00:34:41.787
So we had an average launch
of about 25 sessions per

00:34:41.788 --> 00:34:46.234
week in an average time of
one minute per session.

00:34:46.235 --> 00:34:50.361
So people really were going to
these apps multiple times per

00:34:50.362 --> 00:34:53.095
day, using them in
very short bursts.

00:34:53.096 --> 00:34:56.219
And that that use was sustained
over this eight week period.

00:34:56.220 --> 00:34:59.442
In most mental health apps, you
see a pattern where people might

00:34:59.443 --> 00:35:02.501
use it for a week or two, and
then it drops off dramatically.

00:35:02.502 --> 00:35:05.836
They call this the law
of attrition in digital

00:35:05.837 --> 00:35:07.250
health research.

00:35:07.251 --> 00:35:09.901
That it's pretty inevitable that
people will stop using these

00:35:09.902 --> 00:35:10.707
things over time.

00:35:10.708 --> 00:35:13.606
So at least this was sort of
good evidence that in this,

00:35:13.607 --> 00:35:15.316
again, it's a coached trial.

00:35:15.317 --> 00:35:16.822
So there is a human
supporter there,

00:35:16.823 --> 00:35:19.790
this isn't people who're just
using things on their own.

00:35:19.791 --> 00:35:22.314
We've done some evaluations
about uncoached, and

00:35:22.315 --> 00:35:23.582
it's not as good as this.

00:35:23.583 --> 00:35:26.888
But it still looks fairly good
in the Intellicare on the suite.

00:35:26.889 --> 00:35:29.723
So this is just another thing
about treatment engagement.

00:35:29.724 --> 00:35:33.646
The thing I think I wanna point
out here is that the average

00:35:33.647 --> 00:35:37.735
time that the coaches used was
39 minutes over those eight

00:35:37.736 --> 00:35:38.340
weeks.

00:35:38.341 --> 00:35:41.242
So again, this is a very sort of
lean protocol that these coaches

00:35:41.243 --> 00:35:41.788
are doing.

00:35:41.789 --> 00:35:44.990
This is not full sort of
therapy that's done through

00:35:44.991 --> 00:35:46.089
text messaging.

00:35:46.090 --> 00:35:50.109
It's about three text messages
per week that are sent to

00:35:50.110 --> 00:35:53.359
the user to sort of
encourage them to continue

00:35:53.360 --> 00:35:55.425
to use the platform, yeah.

00:35:55.426 --> 00:35:57.457
&gt;&gt; Are you gonna enter
those notifications or

00:35:57.458 --> 00:35:58.365
emails every day?

00:35:58.366 --> 00:36:03.503
&gt;&gt; So there's some
notifications through the apps.

00:36:03.504 --> 00:36:05.519
It's organized by,

00:36:05.520 --> 00:36:10.450
we have a hub app that
actually provides messaging.

00:36:10.451 --> 00:36:13.740
And so all those notifications
hit the central hub, so

00:36:13.741 --> 00:36:16.460
they're all kind of
contained within the app.

00:36:16.461 --> 00:36:18.840
Another thing actually,
within that hub app,

00:36:18.841 --> 00:36:21.380
is one thing thatt we're really
interested in, is if we can use

00:36:21.381 --> 00:36:24.220
this IntelliCare platform
as a recommendation engine.

00:36:24.221 --> 00:36:27.200
And so, one of the arms in that,

00:36:27.201 --> 00:36:30.560
so I'll say that four arm field
trial is a two by two design.

00:36:30.561 --> 00:36:33.550
So we compared coaching
versus no coaching.

00:36:33.551 --> 00:36:37.670
And we compared a full
IntelliCare hub app that had

00:36:37.671 --> 00:36:40.920
notifications and
recommendations.

00:36:40.921 --> 00:36:44.024
So every week, you would get
a new recommendation that says

00:36:44.025 --> 00:36:46.954
this week, try out these two
IntelliCare apps for you.

00:36:46.955 --> 00:36:51.784
Versus an IntelliCare app, a hub
app that just had the sort of

00:36:51.785 --> 00:36:55.494
available IntelliCare
apps within that hub.

00:36:55.495 --> 00:36:58.633
So no notifications and
no recommendations.

00:36:58.634 --> 00:37:02.569
So one of the things that
we're really interested in is,

00:37:02.570 --> 00:37:04.866
can we actually take these apps?

00:37:04.867 --> 00:37:07.950
And then figure out which
app should the person use in

00:37:07.951 --> 00:37:08.870
the next week?

00:37:08.871 --> 00:37:11.752
So this is just a little
representation of what that

00:37:11.753 --> 00:37:13.434
IntelliCare hub looks like.

00:37:13.435 --> 00:37:17.900
This is the non-recommending
version of the hub app.

00:37:17.901 --> 00:37:19.496
So in the recommending version,

00:37:19.497 --> 00:37:21.719
you'll have a little box
here that would say,

00:37:21.720 --> 00:37:23.999
these are the two apps
you should use this week.

00:37:24.000 --> 00:37:27.455
So right now, it looks like
initial data suggesting that

00:37:27.456 --> 00:37:31.416
we're actually able to increase
use of the applications through

00:37:31.417 --> 00:37:33.040
these recommendations.

00:37:33.041 --> 00:37:37.231
And that the recommendation
engine that we're using is

00:37:37.232 --> 00:37:40.620
actually improving on
a naive recommender,

00:37:40.621 --> 00:37:44.197
that's just giving
random recommendations.

00:37:44.198 --> 00:37:47.016
So it does seem like we are able
to do a little bit of this

00:37:47.017 --> 00:37:48.029
recommendation.

00:37:48.030 --> 00:37:50.483
But I think this is something
where we have a lot of kind of

00:37:50.484 --> 00:37:51.958
room to improve in our platform.

00:37:51.959 --> 00:37:53.001
&gt;&gt; One other question.

00:37:53.002 --> 00:37:53.518
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:37:53.519 --> 00:37:56.345
&gt;&gt; When you are recommending
[INAUDIBLE].

00:37:56.346 --> 00:37:58.017
I mean obviously,
you're recommending and

00:37:58.018 --> 00:37:59.163
prescribing them to use this.

00:37:59.164 --> 00:38:01.646
And that other arm where you
just gave them coached or

00:38:01.647 --> 00:38:03.518
non-coached, do
they get to choose?

00:38:03.519 --> 00:38:05.755
Was there any choice with
using a computer, or?

00:38:05.756 --> 00:38:06.578
&gt;&gt; Yep, yes, exactly.

00:38:06.579 --> 00:38:09.821
Well, even in the recommended
one they can use other apps too.

00:38:09.822 --> 00:38:12.238
So it's not that those are the
only ones available to them.

00:38:12.239 --> 00:38:15.965
We're just pushing them
towards particular ones.

00:38:15.966 --> 00:38:18.677
And these apps are all
freely available on

00:38:18.678 --> 00:38:20.233
the Google Play Store.

00:38:20.234 --> 00:38:23.058
A couple of them are in the iOS,
we've either got,

00:38:23.059 --> 00:38:26.110
think we've got three to five
that are in iOS already.

00:38:26.111 --> 00:38:28.250
I know the development is
kind of rolling out for

00:38:28.251 --> 00:38:30.150
all of the iOS ones.

00:38:30.151 --> 00:38:32.630
We do find that people
from the public store,

00:38:32.631 --> 00:38:36.470
who download the hub tend to
download more applications than

00:38:36.471 --> 00:38:38.430
people who don't
download the hub too.

00:38:38.431 --> 00:38:41.750
So this hub points people to
things that they wouldn't be

00:38:41.751 --> 00:38:43.003
finding otherwise.

00:38:43.004 --> 00:38:45.800
Just looking at IntelliCare
apps, generally,

00:38:45.801 --> 00:38:47.032
from the Play Store.

00:38:47.033 --> 00:38:49.674
&gt;&gt; Has anybody had support
from a healthcare provider in

00:38:49.675 --> 00:38:50.496
recommending or

00:38:50.497 --> 00:38:52.177
kind of influencing [CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; So

00:38:52.178 --> 00:38:55.540
we have not tested it within
a healthcare system yet.

00:38:55.541 --> 00:38:59.290
That's a project we have on the
horizon is actually doing this

00:38:59.291 --> 00:39:00.240
in healthcare settings.

00:39:00.241 --> 00:39:01.784
Where your providers, or

00:39:01.785 --> 00:39:05.091
a member of your provider
team would be that supporter.

00:39:05.092 --> 00:39:06.179
So far, supporters,

00:39:06.180 --> 00:39:09.480
we start out with clinical
psychologists from our group.

00:39:09.481 --> 00:39:11.430
We moved to research assistants,
because as I said,

00:39:11.431 --> 00:39:16.690
we're not doing very fancy stuff
with the recommendations here.

00:39:16.691 --> 00:39:19.620
I mean, one thing we're also
really interested in is if we

00:39:19.621 --> 00:39:22.160
could automate some of those
recommendations through using

00:39:22.161 --> 00:39:23.730
natural language processing.

00:39:23.731 --> 00:39:26.683
Because at least our reports
from the people doing

00:39:26.684 --> 00:39:27.947
the support is that,

00:39:27.948 --> 00:39:31.757
what they're doing is not stuff
that's all that challenging.

00:39:31.758 --> 00:39:33.869
But yeah, I think these are a
lot of open questions we have

00:39:33.870 --> 00:39:35.591
with how we might be able
to use this platform.

00:39:38.176 --> 00:39:43.090
So I wanna move on to another
sort of pilot study that we've

00:39:43.091 --> 00:39:48.107
been working on in Chicago aimed
at providing mental health

00:39:48.108 --> 00:39:53.224
resources to homeless youth,
so Every year about 2 million

00:39:53.225 --> 00:39:59.020
folks ages kind of 18 through
24 will spend a night homeless.

00:39:59.021 --> 00:40:04.240
And the rates of mental
health disorders and

00:40:04.241 --> 00:40:06.780
homeless individuals
are about two to three

00:40:06.781 --> 00:40:09.100
times higher than
the general population.

00:40:09.101 --> 00:40:12.232
At the same time, only about 10%
of those folks get any sort of

00:40:12.233 --> 00:40:14.370
access to mental
health resources.

00:40:14.371 --> 00:40:18.939
So we have a highly vulnerable
population, huge sort of need

00:40:18.940 --> 00:40:23.880
for mental health services, very
little sort of availability.

00:40:23.881 --> 00:40:26.809
So what we've done in Chicago is
we've launched a project that we

00:40:26.810 --> 00:40:28.280
call Stepping Stone.

00:40:28.281 --> 00:40:30.400
Stepping Stone has a couple
of different components.

00:40:30.401 --> 00:40:32.789
First, every youth who
signs up for our study,

00:40:32.790 --> 00:40:35.718
we give them a smartphone device
that's theirs to keep and

00:40:35.719 --> 00:40:37.950
we pay for six months
of their data service.

00:40:39.260 --> 00:40:42.850
It's one thing that we
feel very strongly is that

00:40:42.851 --> 00:40:47.050
keeping them connected to having
technology as a resource is

00:40:47.051 --> 00:40:48.150
very beneficial for them.

00:40:48.151 --> 00:40:51.500
This has been
an interesting challenge.

00:40:51.501 --> 00:40:54.293
We've learned a lot of sort
of interesting things.

00:40:54.294 --> 00:40:56.793
So we give them a data
plan of five gigabytes.

00:40:56.794 --> 00:40:59.890
They get throttled if
they go above that.

00:41:00.910 --> 00:41:03.520
We had one period where our
provider was switching over

00:41:03.521 --> 00:41:05.600
carriers, and
that throttling was turned off.

00:41:05.601 --> 00:41:08.380
We had a youth that used 120
gigabytes of data in one week.

00:41:10.750 --> 00:41:11.370
It was crazy.

00:41:11.371 --> 00:41:14.725
I mean, they're never on Wi-Fi.

00:41:14.726 --> 00:41:16.830
They're using this as
their entertainment,

00:41:16.831 --> 00:41:19.950
as their communication,
as everything.

00:41:19.951 --> 00:41:22.430
It's been a very interesting
learning experience.

00:41:22.431 --> 00:41:26.030
In addition, we put three
applications on their phone.

00:41:26.031 --> 00:41:27.650
We used two of those
IntelliCare apps.

00:41:27.651 --> 00:41:30.850
One focused on meditation and
mindfulness called Purple Chill.

00:41:30.851 --> 00:41:35.320
Another one focused on sleep
hygiene called Slumber Time.

00:41:35.321 --> 00:41:38.030
We have a daily survey
app on their phone called

00:41:38.031 --> 00:41:39.340
Pocket Helper.

00:41:39.341 --> 00:41:42.200
It sends a survey that asks
them about what their biggest

00:41:42.201 --> 00:41:44.600
challenge was that day,
what's their stress level like,

00:41:44.601 --> 00:41:46.670
what was their
sleep quality like.

00:41:46.671 --> 00:41:49.770
And it feeds that information
back to a therapist in our team.

00:41:49.771 --> 00:41:53.746
That therapist, they set up
three 30 minute phone calls that

00:41:53.747 --> 00:41:56.530
focus around kinda
general coping skills.

00:41:56.531 --> 00:41:58.860
They also have the opportunity
to text message that therapist

00:41:58.861 --> 00:42:00.730
at times outside of
those phone calls.

00:42:00.731 --> 00:42:03.587
That's one reason why we're
collecting this daily survey

00:42:03.588 --> 00:42:06.156
information so that if they
text or call the therapist

00:42:06.157 --> 00:42:09.127
kinda outside of the scheduled
sessions, that the therapist

00:42:09.128 --> 00:42:12.060
knows a little bit more about
what's going on in their life.

00:42:12.061 --> 00:42:16.170
And that's what
the project consists of.

00:42:16.171 --> 00:42:19.050
This is what sort of that
daily survey looks like.

00:42:19.051 --> 00:42:23.019
It gives them a coping kind of
tip every day that they rate on

00:42:23.020 --> 00:42:24.400
a five point scale.

00:42:24.401 --> 00:42:27.758
I think, it feeds back their
survey data to them and

00:42:27.759 --> 00:42:30.500
we ask them kinda
those brief questions.

00:42:32.090 --> 00:42:33.600
So this is sort of
the general model.

00:42:33.601 --> 00:42:36.140
We have a back end dashboard
that provides that information

00:42:36.141 --> 00:42:37.480
to the clinician.

00:42:37.481 --> 00:42:40.210
The clinician gets that
information and they are able to

00:42:40.211 --> 00:42:43.140
intervene if something comes up,
to send kind of text message

00:42:43.141 --> 00:42:47.040
support in the moment or as
close to the moment as possible.

00:42:47.041 --> 00:42:49.250
And then the youth are able to
text back to our therapist.

00:42:51.190 --> 00:42:53.824
So we've run this
project now with about,

00:42:53.825 --> 00:42:57.640
I think we have now about 40
people who've been recruited.

00:42:59.882 --> 00:43:02.290
Most people really
like the study.

00:43:02.291 --> 00:43:03.820
They like the tips the best.

00:43:03.821 --> 00:43:05.710
They like the apps the least.

00:43:05.711 --> 00:43:07.600
This is something that's been
really interesting to us,

00:43:07.601 --> 00:43:09.750
because the tips are really
easy for us to develop.

00:43:09.751 --> 00:43:11.670
The apps are very expensive and
hard.

00:43:11.671 --> 00:43:13.728
And so, it makes me
wonder sometimes like,

00:43:13.729 --> 00:43:15.080
why are we building apps?

00:43:15.081 --> 00:43:17.530
Why don't we just
send tips to people?

00:43:17.531 --> 00:43:18.680
It's a good question.

00:43:18.681 --> 00:43:21.770
Most people would recommend
the study for other people.

00:43:21.771 --> 00:43:26.220
We haven't looked at
the clinical outcomes yet, but

00:43:26.221 --> 00:43:29.280
we've had sort of encouraging
results from the engagement.

00:43:29.281 --> 00:43:30.503
Actually, initially,

00:43:30.504 --> 00:43:32.630
our engagement looked
really bi-modal.

00:43:32.631 --> 00:43:34.592
People would either
engage in everything.

00:43:34.593 --> 00:43:35.645
They would complete
all their sessions.

00:43:35.646 --> 00:43:37.677
They would text
message regularly or

00:43:37.678 --> 00:43:40.050
they would kind of
disappear completely.

00:43:40.051 --> 00:43:42.380
We've actually
recently sort of had

00:43:42.381 --> 00:43:44.450
one more sort of
bucket of engagement.

00:43:44.451 --> 00:43:47.553
So we have the people
who don't engage at all.

00:43:47.554 --> 00:43:50.170
We have the people who complete
all their phone sessions and

00:43:50.171 --> 00:43:51.330
text message regularly.

00:43:51.331 --> 00:43:54.390
And now we have a group of folks
who aren't completing any of

00:43:54.391 --> 00:43:55.190
the phone sessions,

00:43:55.191 --> 00:43:57.670
but they're texting regularly
with the therapist.

00:43:57.671 --> 00:44:01.090
So it seems like there's
different ways to engage people

00:44:01.091 --> 00:44:01.900
in these platforms, and

00:44:01.901 --> 00:44:03.440
I think that that's
an important thing to remember,

00:44:03.441 --> 00:44:04.870
is like what is
the expected use.

00:44:04.871 --> 00:44:07.345
And also what are patterns
of engagement that might be

00:44:07.346 --> 00:44:09.654
interesting and useful for
the person doing it.

00:44:09.655 --> 00:44:12.365
But I think a lot of time when
we look at digital mental

00:44:12.366 --> 00:44:14.805
health, people think
about adherence.

00:44:14.806 --> 00:44:17.287
They use that word a lot and I'm
a little skittish with the word

00:44:17.288 --> 00:44:19.438
adherence, because adherence
kind of suggests to me

00:44:19.439 --> 00:44:21.043
that there's one
way of interacting.

00:44:21.044 --> 00:44:22.344
It's like we have a medication,

00:44:22.345 --> 00:44:24.573
you're supposed to use it
three times a day, I'm like,

00:44:24.574 --> 00:44:27.010
I don't know if digital mental
health is the same sort of way.

00:44:27.011 --> 00:44:29.260
So I really like to think about
this as sort of engagement or

00:44:29.261 --> 00:44:29.810
usage.

00:44:29.811 --> 00:44:32.400
But there's different ways
that people might engage with

00:44:32.401 --> 00:44:33.060
the platform.

00:44:33.061 --> 00:44:36.274
But then it doesn't mean
that one way is necessarily

00:44:36.275 --> 00:44:37.150
the best way or

00:44:37.151 --> 00:44:41.180
we should preference one style
of engagement besides the other.

00:44:41.181 --> 00:44:42.910
I think we need to learn
more about what styles of

00:44:42.911 --> 00:44:44.960
engagement are useful for
different folks.

00:44:44.961 --> 00:44:48.131
&gt;&gt; Why did they like
the apps the least?

00:44:48.132 --> 00:44:50.499
&gt;&gt; I think one of the reasons
is that they weren't

00:44:50.500 --> 00:44:53.000
well-integrated into
the program that we had.

00:44:53.001 --> 00:44:55.847
I also think that the sleep
app we put on there,

00:44:55.848 --> 00:44:59.791
because that was one of our sort
of first goals for this project,

00:44:59.792 --> 00:45:01.950
was to work with sleep hygiene.

00:45:01.951 --> 00:45:03.680
And the kids are actually
not all that

00:45:03.681 --> 00:45:04.800
interested in sleep hygiene.

00:45:04.801 --> 00:45:06.860
There's a lot of other things
that they have going on.

00:45:06.861 --> 00:45:10.230
I think, there are problems
with depression anxiety,

00:45:10.231 --> 00:45:12.460
also slightly different
than in general population.

00:45:12.461 --> 00:45:15.760
So, there's a lot of sort
of emotional regulation in

00:45:15.761 --> 00:45:17.170
interpersonal issues
that come up.

00:45:17.171 --> 00:45:20.070
Those are the main content
that kinda came up

00:45:20.071 --> 00:45:21.800
in the text messages
in the fund sessions.

00:45:21.801 --> 00:45:24.100
And we don't have apps
that kind of specifically,

00:45:24.101 --> 00:45:25.010
address those things.

00:45:25.011 --> 00:45:27.735
And so, even though the sort
of treatment, like cognitive

00:45:27.736 --> 00:45:30.514
behavioral therapy would still
be a treatment strategy that

00:45:30.515 --> 00:45:31.341
would be useful.

00:45:31.342 --> 00:45:33.029
We might need to
develop apps that kind

00:45:33.030 --> 00:45:35.298
of focus on the specific
content areas that would be

00:45:35.299 --> 00:45:36.660
interesting to those group.

00:45:36.661 --> 00:45:38.750
So I think, tailoring for

00:45:38.751 --> 00:45:41.780
particular population is sort
of really key and critical.

00:45:41.781 --> 00:45:43.589
&gt;&gt; One last thing on that.

00:45:43.590 --> 00:45:45.559
I was just curious about
what apps you decided,

00:45:45.560 --> 00:45:48.520
how you go about deciding what
apps you are going to put on?

00:45:48.521 --> 00:45:51.460
&gt;&gt; So one, was because
our funder was funding to

00:45:51.461 --> 00:45:54.270
do sort of sleep stuff, and so
we put the sleep app on there.

00:45:54.271 --> 00:45:56.050
We're always tied a little
bit by where our money is

00:45:56.051 --> 00:45:56.570
coming from.

00:45:56.571 --> 00:45:58.530
And then, the Mindfulness app,

00:45:58.531 --> 00:46:01.120
we put on there because it
reinforced a lot of the coping

00:46:01.121 --> 00:46:05.580
skills that we were teaching
in those fund sessions.

00:46:05.581 --> 00:46:08.258
That is actually something
we are moving to a phase two

00:46:08.259 --> 00:46:09.500
project here.

00:46:09.501 --> 00:46:10.624
And in the phase two,

00:46:10.625 --> 00:46:13.938
we are putting more diversity of
apps on there, because we have

00:46:13.939 --> 00:46:16.580
the apps available to us
in the healthcare suite.

00:46:16.581 --> 00:46:18.580
It might make sense just to
give them all to them, and

00:46:18.581 --> 00:46:19.564
see which ones they use.

00:46:19.565 --> 00:46:23.467
So, here's actually, I wanna
conclude real fast with a kind

00:46:23.468 --> 00:46:26.854
of conceptual thing that I
talk a lot about in my work.

00:46:26.855 --> 00:46:29.553
That I think I wanna frame
a little bit differently here,

00:46:29.554 --> 00:46:32.589
because being here at Microsoft,
I know that this might be a term

00:46:32.590 --> 00:46:34.348
that some people
are familiar with.

00:46:34.349 --> 00:46:35.651
And I use it slightly different

00:46:35.652 --> 00:46:37.312
than it's used in
common literature.

00:46:37.313 --> 00:46:42.287
So the term skeuomorphism is a
term that's used a lot in UI/UX

00:46:42.288 --> 00:46:43.460
design.

00:46:43.461 --> 00:46:47.889
To talk about visual elements
that exist in digital

00:46:47.890 --> 00:46:52.422
technologies that are remnants
of the past physical

00:46:52.423 --> 00:46:54.080
examples of that.

00:46:54.081 --> 00:46:57.139
So, I used this example here of
light bulbs that looked like

00:46:57.140 --> 00:46:57.995
candle flames,

00:46:57.996 --> 00:47:00.818
because that's what candles
looked like in the past.

00:47:00.819 --> 00:47:03.398
Those things could be anything,
they could be dinosaurs,

00:47:03.399 --> 00:47:04.636
they could be hockey pucks,

00:47:04.637 --> 00:47:06.920
but we decided to make them
candle to look like fire.

00:47:06.921 --> 00:47:08.940
This is a great example I
got from a presentation,

00:47:08.941 --> 00:47:09.806
where someone says,

00:47:09.807 --> 00:47:11.744
I showed my 12 year old
son an old floppy disk.

00:47:11.745 --> 00:47:14.690
And he said, wow, cool,
you 3D printed the save icon.

00:47:14.691 --> 00:47:18.236
No, that's the opposite way that
works, we made a save icon that

00:47:18.237 --> 00:47:20.891
represented the way that
we used to save things.

00:47:20.892 --> 00:47:23.878
So, I think that often in
digital mental health,

00:47:23.879 --> 00:47:26.970
we actually engage in
conceptual skeumorphisms.

00:47:26.971 --> 00:47:30.797
Where we take concepts from
face to face therapy, and

00:47:30.798 --> 00:47:34.040
we translate them to
digital technologies.

00:47:34.041 --> 00:47:37.646
So we make web-based cognitive
behavior therapy programs,

00:47:37.647 --> 00:47:40.780
where we expect people to
come back on a weekly basis.

00:47:40.781 --> 00:47:43.680
And spend 50 minutes
going through content,

00:47:43.681 --> 00:47:45.830
because that's what
psychotherapy is like.

00:47:45.831 --> 00:47:48.433
When, that's not the way
that most people use digital

00:47:48.434 --> 00:47:49.119
technology.

00:47:49.120 --> 00:47:49.758
And that maybe is

00:47:49.759 --> 00:47:51.525
not the way that will be
most helpful to them.

00:47:51.526 --> 00:47:55.355
And so, effectively, we
are trying to fit our old model

00:47:55.356 --> 00:47:59.281
of psychological practice
into these digital mediums.

00:47:59.282 --> 00:48:02.171
And I think, instead what
we should be doing is

00:48:02.172 --> 00:48:05.804
we think about digital mental
health is re-imagining how

00:48:05.805 --> 00:48:09.521
we can actually take unique
affordances of technologies.

00:48:09.522 --> 00:48:13.987
To build new products, new
designs that actually leverage

00:48:13.988 --> 00:48:16.845
what we know about
behavior change and

00:48:16.846 --> 00:48:19.350
mental health strategies.

00:48:19.351 --> 00:48:23.100
But do so in sort of the new
medium in technology.

00:48:23.101 --> 00:48:25.504
So, here's actually a project
that I did with Alana Yorosh

00:48:25.505 --> 00:48:26.955
from the University
of Minnesota.

00:48:26.956 --> 00:48:30.681
Where we were sort of very
interesting the stakeholder

00:48:30.682 --> 00:48:34.409
engage perspective around
happiness technologies.

00:48:34.410 --> 00:48:38.963
So we did a six week, sorry,

00:48:38.964 --> 00:48:43.330
yeah, six week cooperative
inquiry, sort of co-design

00:48:43.331 --> 00:48:47.460
workshop with kids around
happiness technologies.

00:48:47.461 --> 00:48:50.060
So this was a true sort of
interdisciplinary project,

00:48:50.061 --> 00:48:52.717
I came in as the clinical
psychologist with a background

00:48:52.718 --> 00:48:53.973
in positive psychology.

00:48:53.974 --> 00:48:56.265
We spent the first couple of
weeks of the workshop teaching

00:48:56.266 --> 00:48:57.680
kids about
the inventing process.

00:48:57.681 --> 00:49:00.808
And also, teaching them
process psychology skills, so

00:49:00.809 --> 00:49:03.870
I did what, I will typically
do as a psychologist if I was

00:49:03.871 --> 00:49:05.968
running just
a psychology workshop.

00:49:05.969 --> 00:49:09.026
And then we transitioned to,
okay, now that we've empowered

00:49:09.027 --> 00:49:11.515
you with the concept and
the knowledge of.

00:49:11.516 --> 00:49:13.304
We talked to you
about gratitude,

00:49:13.305 --> 00:49:16.018
we talked to you about
mindfulness, we talked to you

00:49:16.019 --> 00:49:19.119
about some problem solving
from a cognitive perspective.

00:49:19.120 --> 00:49:22.846
What sort of technologies would
you guys build that would be

00:49:22.847 --> 00:49:24.720
able to promote well being?

00:49:24.721 --> 00:49:27.909
And if it came up with some
really sort of interesting

00:49:27.910 --> 00:49:28.635
examples.

00:49:28.636 --> 00:49:29.878
This is my favorite one here,

00:49:29.879 --> 00:49:32.300
this is the mindfulness
backpack.

00:49:32.301 --> 00:49:35.920
It had a little sort of
distraction machine on the back,

00:49:35.921 --> 00:49:39.055
to be able to take people out
of whatever moment they're in.

00:49:39.056 --> 00:49:40.844
It also had a good
smell generator.

00:49:40.845 --> 00:49:44.501
And the kinda good smells these
kids came up with were chocolate

00:49:44.502 --> 00:49:47.196
chip cookies, and
the smell of their own bed.

00:49:47.197 --> 00:49:49.544
Just really sort of
cool creative things.

00:49:49.545 --> 00:49:51.659
They also came up with things
that were very similar to

00:49:51.660 --> 00:49:53.169
some things that
have been designed.

00:49:53.170 --> 00:49:56.262
So this was their sort of
thought tank that used fish,

00:49:56.263 --> 00:49:59.835
which I think is kind of funny,
because I've seen a lot of sort

00:49:59.836 --> 00:50:03.137
of designs from different folks
where they use animals or

00:50:03.138 --> 00:50:06.369
fish as kind of representations
of mental sort of health

00:50:06.370 --> 00:50:09.776
processes and sort of visual
displays for applications.

00:50:09.777 --> 00:50:13.138
But this one is kind of coming
up with a positive cognitive

00:50:13.139 --> 00:50:13.776
reframe.

00:50:13.777 --> 00:50:15.676
So the fish here are saying,
I'm smart.

00:50:15.677 --> 00:50:19.436
Kind of counteracting some
negative thoughts that they had.

00:50:19.437 --> 00:50:22.521
But it was really sort of
interesting, engaging, and

00:50:22.522 --> 00:50:25.217
useful to engage in this
process with the kids.

00:50:25.218 --> 00:50:29.019
Another thing I thought that was
really interesting was looking

00:50:29.020 --> 00:50:31.910
at the engagement strategies
they came up with,

00:50:31.911 --> 00:50:35.291
with how would you want to
entice kids to stay engaged with

00:50:35.292 --> 00:50:37.277
these different technologies?

00:50:37.278 --> 00:50:40.884
We definitely saw the sort of
typical engagement strategies

00:50:40.885 --> 00:50:43.437
that come up in what
a lot of people design.

00:50:43.438 --> 00:50:45.097
Games, gamification.

00:50:45.098 --> 00:50:45.877
Heath is huge.

00:50:45.878 --> 00:50:47.249
Although I have a separate rant,

00:50:47.250 --> 00:50:49.897
I'll tell you another time about
my theories on gamification.

00:50:49.898 --> 00:50:51.332
Social support is big, but

00:50:51.333 --> 00:50:54.580
actually some of the things I
thought were really interesting

00:50:54.581 --> 00:50:57.337
were responding directly
to emotions or thoughts.

00:50:57.338 --> 00:50:59.977
Making these things more
tangible for people.

00:50:59.978 --> 00:51:03.903
It was also really interesting,
if we go back to this example

00:51:03.904 --> 00:51:07.676
here, you can see that most of
the things that the kids came

00:51:07.677 --> 00:51:10.757
up with were some form
of embodied technology.

00:51:10.758 --> 00:51:15.917
Wearables, backpacks,
robots, watches.

00:51:15.918 --> 00:51:18.317
They really wanted something
they could touch and feel.

00:51:18.318 --> 00:51:21.222
If you look at the digital
mental health space,

00:51:21.223 --> 00:51:23.117
most of the stuff is software.

00:51:23.118 --> 00:51:26.468
We have very few wearable,
tangible technologies that

00:51:26.469 --> 00:51:30.051
are trying to promote mental
health, especially in kids.

00:51:31.730 --> 00:51:33.938
I think this is a space that
really would be interesting

00:51:33.939 --> 00:51:34.466
to develop.

00:51:34.467 --> 00:51:37.649
So I think that teaming with
kids gave us some interesting

00:51:37.650 --> 00:51:40.768
ideas about what are different
things that people would

00:51:40.769 --> 00:51:41.847
be interested in.

00:51:41.848 --> 00:51:43.920
I think more of this sort
of co-design process needs

00:51:43.921 --> 00:51:45.387
to be done in digital
mental health.

00:51:45.388 --> 00:51:47.947
Again, I'm probably preaching
to the choir a little bit here.

00:51:47.948 --> 00:51:51.087
But I think coming from a
clinical psychology background,

00:51:51.088 --> 00:51:54.164
this is typically not what
clinicians do when they develop

00:51:54.165 --> 00:51:56.730
new mental health technologies.

00:51:56.731 --> 00:51:59.052
&gt;&gt; A device that slaps you if
you're not being grateful.

00:51:59.053 --> 00:52:00.027
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah,

00:52:00.028 --> 00:52:01.692
there was also sort of this.

00:52:01.693 --> 00:52:04.383
I actually thought this was
a theme that was really sort

00:52:04.384 --> 00:52:07.545
of interesting with kids is that
there was this interesting sort

00:52:07.546 --> 00:52:10.077
of balance between
the positive and the negative.

00:52:10.078 --> 00:52:14.306
And that they wanted things that
sort of identified the negative

00:52:14.307 --> 00:52:17.439
experiences that they
were going through, and

00:52:17.440 --> 00:52:21.217
had some ways to try to
incorporate the positive aspect.

00:52:21.218 --> 00:52:24.511
And so, that's a really sort
of interesting dichotomy that

00:52:24.512 --> 00:52:27.674
doesn't show up in sort of more
broad sort of discussion of

00:52:27.675 --> 00:52:28.977
positive psychology.

00:52:28.978 --> 00:52:31.897
So again, some of these ideas
are sort of off the wall.

00:52:31.898 --> 00:52:34.280
I will say that at least
in the iteration process,

00:52:34.281 --> 00:52:37.061
I think that something that was
sort of unique about the way

00:52:37.062 --> 00:52:39.898
that we did this is we did this
sort of long-term workshop,

00:52:39.899 --> 00:52:42.602
there were 14 sessions that
we did with the same kids.

00:52:42.603 --> 00:52:44.062
They came back.

00:52:44.063 --> 00:52:46.002
They learned more about
the inventing process.

00:52:46.003 --> 00:52:49.720
The amount of ideas they
generated sort of decreased over

00:52:49.721 --> 00:52:52.430
time and the quality
of their ideas tended

00:52:52.431 --> 00:52:54.762
to get better
throughout session.

00:52:54.763 --> 00:52:58.028
So definitely, they learned
along the way how to engage in

00:52:58.029 --> 00:52:59.303
this process with us.

00:52:59.304 --> 00:53:01.799
And I think most of the times,
again,

00:53:01.800 --> 00:53:04.923
I come from a clinical
psychology background.

00:53:04.924 --> 00:53:07.003
I'm not a designer even
though I love this stuff.

00:53:07.004 --> 00:53:10.648
But most of the people I see
doing sort of this type of work

00:53:10.649 --> 00:53:14.138
in psychology tend to do
a workshop with a group of kids

00:53:14.139 --> 00:53:18.038
and move on to another workshop
with another group of kids.

00:53:18.039 --> 00:53:20.938
And I think there is this
sort of interesting aspect

00:53:20.939 --> 00:53:24.514
of trying to think better on how
to team over the long-term with

00:53:24.515 --> 00:53:28.156
the stakeholders that we're
working with, cuz I think that's

00:53:28.157 --> 00:53:30.933
a big part of this sort
of mental health journey.

00:53:30.934 --> 00:53:35.126
Is that we design a lot of these
sort of singular experiences, or

00:53:35.127 --> 00:53:38.633
experiences that are meant
to stay stable over time.

00:53:38.634 --> 00:53:40.006
And that's not the way
therapy works,

00:53:40.007 --> 00:53:41.129
that's not the way life works,

00:53:41.130 --> 00:53:42.593
that's not the way
mental health works.

00:53:42.594 --> 00:53:45.507
And I think we need to better
think about also how we allow

00:53:45.508 --> 00:53:47.181
tools to be flexible
to allow for

00:53:47.182 --> 00:53:49.233
a variety of different
interactions.

00:53:49.234 --> 00:53:51.973
So just a couple of
conclusions here.

00:53:51.974 --> 00:53:54.426
There's lots of mobile
mental health options that

00:53:54.427 --> 00:53:55.173
are out there.

00:53:55.174 --> 00:53:58.433
They're actually being
developed at a breakneck pace.

00:53:58.434 --> 00:54:01.732
Every single day, it seems
like there is some new app or

00:54:01.733 --> 00:54:02.593
new product or

00:54:02.594 --> 00:54:06.493
new something that someone's
doing in digital mental health.

00:54:06.494 --> 00:54:09.831
The diversity of mobile mental
health options is somewhat

00:54:09.832 --> 00:54:10.993
limited, though.

00:54:10.994 --> 00:54:13.296
A lot of people tend to
be building the same sort

00:54:13.297 --> 00:54:13.894
of things.

00:54:13.895 --> 00:54:14.534
I have seen so

00:54:14.535 --> 00:54:17.399
many cognitive behavioral
therapy thought record apps.

00:54:17.400 --> 00:54:20.266
We built a cognitive behavioral
therapy sort of thought

00:54:20.267 --> 00:54:20.939
record app.

00:54:20.940 --> 00:54:22.299
And the quality is unknown.

00:54:22.300 --> 00:54:25.670
And so I think that one of our
goals right now should be to

00:54:25.671 --> 00:54:29.188
introduce some more variance,
some more diversity into

00:54:29.189 --> 00:54:33.019
the products, the designs,
the things we're evaluating.

00:54:33.020 --> 00:54:35.586
And use the information we
have available to us to better

00:54:35.587 --> 00:54:37.498
understand which of
these things work and

00:54:37.499 --> 00:54:38.928
which of these things don't.

00:54:38.929 --> 00:54:42.214
And then again, to kinda go back
to this theme I mentioned at

00:54:42.215 --> 00:54:45.505
the beginning about this
excitement, this enthusiasm for

00:54:45.506 --> 00:54:49.080
digital mental health has not
been matched by implementation.

00:54:49.081 --> 00:54:51.433
We see lots of things that
are out there that have been

00:54:51.434 --> 00:54:52.030
developed.

00:54:52.031 --> 00:54:55.160
We've not seen a lot of things
that have really caught on.

00:54:55.161 --> 00:54:57.589
At least in the area of
kind of depression anxiety,

00:54:57.590 --> 00:55:00.584
there's been somewhere on
the magnitude of 100 randomized

00:55:00.585 --> 00:55:02.800
controlled trials
evaluating these things.

00:55:02.801 --> 00:55:05.900
There's not been one sort of
successful implementation where

00:55:05.901 --> 00:55:08.080
these things have caught on and

00:55:08.081 --> 00:55:09.820
stayed around in
a healthcare system.

00:55:09.821 --> 00:55:13.030
And I think that one of the big
problems for that is these

00:55:13.031 --> 00:55:17.170
things have not been designed
with implementation in mind.

00:55:17.171 --> 00:55:19.810
And we haven't really deeply
thought about how these things

00:55:19.811 --> 00:55:22.384
can integrate into
the healthcare systems that

00:55:22.385 --> 00:55:23.650
they're in.

00:55:23.651 --> 00:55:28.011
These are just some examples
of sort of recent designs, or

00:55:28.012 --> 00:55:32.194
different things in mobile
mental health that have been

00:55:32.195 --> 00:55:33.630
rolled out there.

00:55:33.631 --> 00:55:36.982
And I think one thing that's
been really interesting to me,

00:55:36.983 --> 00:55:40.332
as an observer in this field, is
that we see more and more sort

00:55:40.333 --> 00:55:43.883
of work where we're seeing these
things pop up in places where we

00:55:43.884 --> 00:55:47.060
don't think about these things
as a mental health tool.

00:55:47.061 --> 00:55:51.255
So Facebook's efforts to try to
do suicide identification or

00:55:51.256 --> 00:55:55.960
outreach, the Google calendar
feature a sort of recommended

00:55:55.961 --> 00:55:59.220
goal and times where we have
open spaces in our calendar.

00:55:59.221 --> 00:56:00.690
This is behavior activation.

00:56:00.691 --> 00:56:03.330
This is something we've been
doing in clinical psychology for

00:56:03.331 --> 00:56:04.424
years and years and years.

00:56:04.425 --> 00:56:07.430
You get people more active, and
you get people more engaged and

00:56:07.431 --> 00:56:10.880
naturally reinforcing things,
spending time with other people,

00:56:10.881 --> 00:56:13.540
being physically active, this
will help your mental health.

00:56:13.541 --> 00:56:15.950
And so I think there's
a lot of places where

00:56:17.210 --> 00:56:21.460
clinical psychology ideas
could be well implemented and

00:56:21.461 --> 00:56:24.320
integrated into existing
technologies if it's done

00:56:24.321 --> 00:56:25.950
thoughtfully and
if it's done usefully.

00:56:25.951 --> 00:56:28.600
This kind of goes back to the
theme I sort of mentioned with

00:56:28.601 --> 00:56:31.280
the paper we did,

00:56:31.281 --> 00:56:34.360
reviewing different health apps
that people were using where

00:56:34.361 --> 00:56:39.470
people identified health
uses from non-health apps.

00:56:39.471 --> 00:56:40.335
So in conclusion,

00:56:40.336 --> 00:56:43.198
I just wanna acknowledge many of
the people who helped me do this

00:56:43.199 --> 00:56:45.846
work as well as some of my
different funding organizations

00:56:45.847 --> 00:56:47.427
that have helped
to support this.

00:56:47.428 --> 00:56:50.931
And I think we have a little
bit of time for questions, but

00:56:50.932 --> 00:56:53.997
I do wanna kinda come back
to the question of sort of

00:56:53.998 --> 00:56:56.627
a integration into
healthcare settings.

00:56:56.628 --> 00:56:57.967
But thank you all for
your attention.

00:56:57.968 --> 00:57:03.627
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]

00:57:03.628 --> 00:57:04.227
&gt;&gt; Sure, yeah.

00:57:04.228 --> 00:57:06.148
So I think it's
a really tricky thing.

00:57:06.149 --> 00:57:10.189
There's a project that I've been
working on right now where we

00:57:10.190 --> 00:57:13.926
developed a platform that's
made to be used in the context

00:57:13.927 --> 00:57:17.206
of cognitive behavioral
therapy for depression,

00:57:17.207 --> 00:57:19.798
and we did that through
sort of a usage and

00:57:19.799 --> 00:57:22.595
design with both patients and
providers.

00:57:22.596 --> 00:57:25.920
And those groups have
very different goals and

00:57:25.921 --> 00:57:27.100
very different
sort of interests.

00:57:27.101 --> 00:57:29.830
And so I think,
we need better ways to

00:57:29.831 --> 00:57:34.090
bring the information that
patients or people create into

00:57:34.091 --> 00:57:36.690
the clinical setting in ways
that are usable to clinicians.

00:57:36.691 --> 00:57:39.384
And so there's a project also,
another project I'm working on

00:57:39.385 --> 00:57:42.810
where we're trying to use Fitbit
data in an intensive outpatient

00:57:42.811 --> 00:57:46.060
program for veterans with PTSD

00:57:46.061 --> 00:57:48.210
to try to get information
about activity levels,

00:57:48.211 --> 00:57:50.330
sleep levels that might be
able to inform the treatment.

00:57:50.331 --> 00:57:53.210
But I think what we
really need to know is,

00:57:53.211 --> 00:57:55.310
what are sort of the treatment
goals that providers have and

00:57:55.311 --> 00:57:57.760
how can this information,
this data, be most useful for

00:57:57.761 --> 00:57:58.940
the things that
they're accomplishing.

00:57:58.941 --> 00:58:00.572
So I think it's
a design problem, but

00:58:00.573 --> 00:58:02.360
I think it's one
that's surmountable.

00:58:02.361 --> 00:58:03.688
&gt;&gt; Sounds like a similar shape,

00:58:03.689 --> 00:58:05.919
I thought maybe you'd be
a little more advanced,

00:58:05.920 --> 00:58:08.894
like some cardiovascular health
area because we've been talking

00:58:08.895 --> 00:58:10.131
to providers there, too.

00:58:10.132 --> 00:58:12.927
And a lot of information that
they get at cardiovascular

00:58:12.928 --> 00:58:15.240
health is not usable
to the provider.

00:58:15.241 --> 00:58:17.315
But the patients find
it very interesting.

00:58:17.316 --> 00:58:18.827
Whether it's valid or not,
they still find it interesting.

00:58:18.828 --> 00:58:20.000
[CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; Yes, I think,

00:58:20.001 --> 00:58:22.239
one thing that we found in some
sayings, is that it might be

00:58:22.240 --> 00:58:24.436
the case that we're gonna have
to create new professions

00:58:24.437 --> 00:58:26.670
of people who are going to be
able to deal with that data.

00:58:26.671 --> 00:58:29.742
Cuz again, like
the cardiovascular folks and

00:58:29.743 --> 00:58:32.970
providers usually don't
have a lot of time, but

00:58:32.971 --> 00:58:36.986
if you could have another role
sort of develop that could be,

00:58:36.987 --> 00:58:40.372
sort of the information
collector and presenter,

00:58:40.373 --> 00:58:42.601
I think that might be something.

00:58:42.602 --> 00:58:44.801
&gt;&gt; There would be some
way to provide them that.

00:58:44.802 --> 00:58:47.553
So a physician we were talking
to said the same thing.

00:58:47.554 --> 00:58:49.740
If there were some way to
take that information and

00:58:49.741 --> 00:58:51.773
boil it down to something
useful to a treatment

00:58:51.774 --> 00:58:53.546
plan that a physician
could recommend,

00:58:53.547 --> 00:58:55.598
then maybe they'd have
some use for the data.

00:58:55.599 --> 00:58:58.298
&gt;&gt; Otherwise,
it becomes just noise to them.

00:58:58.299 --> 00:59:00.492
&gt;&gt; Yep, exactly, yep.

00:59:00.493 --> 00:59:01.410
&gt;&gt; Grades.

00:59:01.411 --> 00:59:02.808
&gt;&gt; Sorry?

00:59:02.809 --> 00:59:07.751
&gt;&gt; Well done physicians and
pharmacists and.

00:59:07.752 --> 00:59:08.571
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I've heard that.

00:59:08.572 --> 00:59:10.179
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I don't like that but

00:59:10.180 --> 00:59:12.792
it's an interesting
model about how it goes.

00:59:12.793 --> 00:59:13.392
&gt;&gt; What was it?

00:59:15.851 --> 00:59:16.942
&gt;&gt; I don't know.

00:59:16.943 --> 00:59:18.484
&gt;&gt; So
why don't you like gamification?

00:59:18.485 --> 00:59:21.950
There's such a huge
push [INAUDIBLE].

00:59:21.951 --> 00:59:26.340
&gt;&gt; So I think gamification,
we did have points in this.

00:59:26.341 --> 00:59:31.010
So I think gamification has
to be done very mindfully for

00:59:31.011 --> 00:59:32.530
two aspects.

00:59:32.531 --> 00:59:35.250
One that, if we're dealing
with mental health,

00:59:35.251 --> 00:59:38.350
I think we wanna make sure
that we respect that for

00:59:38.351 --> 00:59:40.770
many people this is a very
serious, very challenging thing.

00:59:40.771 --> 00:59:44.596
And I don't think we don't want
to necessarily transform this

00:59:44.597 --> 00:59:45.902
into a game for them.

00:59:45.903 --> 00:59:48.997
I think the other sort of
aspect, I borrow a phrase from

00:59:48.998 --> 00:59:52.363
Ian Bogus that he talks about
chocolate covered broccoli,

00:59:52.364 --> 00:59:54.515
is that So
we know chocolate's good,

00:59:54.516 --> 00:59:56.419
we know broccoli's good for us.

00:59:56.420 --> 00:59:58.025
Most of us are willing
to tolerate broccoli

00:59:58.026 --> 01:00:00.550
because we know it's good for
us even if we don't love it.

01:00:00.551 --> 01:00:02.160
We love chocolate, but

01:00:02.161 --> 01:00:04.600
you don't want to put
those two things together.

01:00:04.601 --> 01:00:06.330
And so I think gamification
is that sometimes.

01:00:06.331 --> 01:00:07.550
It's like,
we know health is good for us,

01:00:07.551 --> 01:00:08.880
we know these things are good.

01:00:08.881 --> 01:00:10.900
We know games are fun and
we like to do them.

01:00:10.901 --> 01:00:12.920
But you don't wanna just
spread the games all over

01:00:12.921 --> 01:00:14.510
the health stuff.

01:00:14.511 --> 01:00:18.710
So I think that if you, one,
sort of belittle a person's

01:00:18.711 --> 01:00:23.108
emotional experience in
the context of mental disorders.

01:00:23.109 --> 01:00:26.547
And two, make it more about
the extrinsic engagement in

01:00:26.548 --> 01:00:30.645
the gaming component, as opposed
to the intrinsic value that they

01:00:30.646 --> 01:00:34.397
get out of the activity,
I think that's very challenging.

01:00:34.398 --> 01:00:38.450
At the same time, we know game
mechanics, we know challenges.

01:00:38.451 --> 01:00:42.120
That's a big part of
sort of mental health.

01:00:42.121 --> 01:00:45.120
Something I say all the time
is that one of the secrets to

01:00:45.121 --> 01:00:47.410
really getting people hooked
into the things that we do or

01:00:47.411 --> 01:00:50.000
getting people moving in
the right direction is

01:00:50.001 --> 01:00:51.830
clear goals and
immediate feedback.

01:00:51.831 --> 01:00:53.360
And that's a game.

01:00:53.361 --> 01:00:57.095
So I think that we can
integrate gaming elements, but

01:00:57.096 --> 01:01:00.251
the term gamification
scares me sometimes.

01:01:00.252 --> 01:01:03.600
So that's my gamification rant,
chocolate covered brocoli.

01:01:03.601 --> 01:01:06.189
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah?

01:01:06.190 --> 01:01:08.393
&gt;&gt; I have a two-part
question about PsyberGuide.

01:01:08.394 --> 01:01:11.560
How do you choose which
apps you wanna evaluate?

01:01:11.561 --> 01:01:14.612
Second is, you mentioned that
the scores are different for

01:01:14.613 --> 01:01:17.370
your PsyberGuide ratings and
the App Store ratings.

01:01:17.371 --> 01:01:19.121
I was wondering what is
the correlation between

01:01:19.122 --> 01:01:19.741
those ratings?

01:01:19.742 --> 01:01:22.148
&gt;&gt; So I think the correlation
there is about 0.15.

01:01:22.149 --> 01:01:23.440
I would have to
look it up again.

01:01:23.441 --> 01:01:25.559
It's pretty low.

01:01:25.560 --> 01:01:26.623
So I answered that first cuz

01:01:26.624 --> 01:01:28.490
it's the easier
question to answer.

01:01:28.491 --> 01:01:29.990
In terms of prioritizing apps,

01:01:29.991 --> 01:01:34.300
we have sort of a multi-stage
prioritization process.

01:01:34.301 --> 01:01:37.700
We do a consistent review of
the scientific literature,

01:01:37.701 --> 01:01:40.270
again, because one
of the sort of

01:01:40.271 --> 01:01:43.240
the aspects we're rating
things on is credibility.

01:01:43.241 --> 01:01:45.370
We know that things are gonna
get higher credibility scores if

01:01:45.371 --> 01:01:47.714
they're in the scientific
literature and

01:01:47.715 --> 01:01:50.250
so we try to sort
of promote those.

01:01:50.251 --> 01:01:53.260
We then do sort of
a prioritization based on

01:01:54.390 --> 01:01:57.420
a popularity score
that's a combination of

01:01:57.421 --> 01:02:01.090
the number of downloads and the
reviews from the public stores.

01:02:01.091 --> 01:02:04.260
As well as sort of
identification through

01:02:04.261 --> 01:02:07.080
searches and other
appearances in media sources.

01:02:07.081 --> 01:02:08.748
So if people
are talking about it,

01:02:08.749 --> 01:02:10.884
we wanna make sure
that it's represented.

01:02:10.885 --> 01:02:12.513
If there's an app that's
up there that's not

01:02:12.514 --> 01:02:14.435
really being used,
people aren't talking about it,

01:02:14.436 --> 01:02:15.790
it's not as important for
us to rate.

01:02:17.490 --> 01:02:21.440
Especially if we don't have good
evidence that has scientific

01:02:21.441 --> 01:02:24.428
support or sort of clinical
content behind it.

01:02:24.429 --> 01:02:27.749
So those are sort of the main
factors we use to sort of

01:02:27.750 --> 01:02:29.770
identify and to propose them.

01:02:29.771 --> 01:02:32.833
We also get some sort of
self-referrals through

01:02:32.834 --> 01:02:35.603
a developer, his design and
application.

01:02:35.604 --> 01:02:37.300
They want it redone
in Psyberguide.

01:02:37.301 --> 01:02:42.123
And so I think we get, depending
on the week, five to ten people

01:02:42.124 --> 01:02:45.763
contacting us about,
would you post my thing or

01:02:45.764 --> 01:02:50.138
would you evaluate this thing
that I've seen out there?

01:02:50.139 --> 01:02:52.576
I think a lot of times we get
things of people who have

01:02:52.577 --> 01:02:54.564
created some sort of
self-help site that

01:02:54.565 --> 01:02:57.730
has just content on it, which is
not the sort of stuff we review.

01:02:57.731 --> 01:03:03.490
And we also get a lot
of requests from B2B or

01:03:03.491 --> 01:03:04.920
to business products.

01:03:04.921 --> 01:03:08.710
So platforms are trying to sell
to healthcare settings or to

01:03:08.711 --> 01:03:11.410
businesses to provide some sort
of mental health support for

01:03:11.411 --> 01:03:14.240
their employees or
the folks that they cover.

01:03:14.241 --> 01:03:17.320
We currently are not evaluating
those because we're meant

01:03:17.321 --> 01:03:21.178
to be a more
consumer-facing website.

01:03:21.179 --> 01:03:24.353
And then the other reason is
that a lot of businesses and

01:03:24.354 --> 01:03:27.875
healthcare systems have the
resources to evaluate things,

01:03:27.876 --> 01:03:31.203
and usually have their own
sort of evaluation criteria.

01:03:31.204 --> 01:03:33.374
And so we're really trying
to provide a service for

01:03:33.375 --> 01:03:35.860
people who don't have the
resources to evaluate things.

01:03:35.861 --> 01:03:36.980
Or again, to kind of go back to

01:03:36.981 --> 01:03:39.400
the American Psychiatric
Association framework,

01:03:39.401 --> 01:03:41.310
maybe don't know how to
evaluate it themselves,

01:03:41.311 --> 01:03:42.890
even if they're given
a little bit of a framework.

01:03:42.891 --> 01:03:46.276
So we're trying to create
more information for

01:03:46.277 --> 01:03:47.974
the people who need it.

01:03:47.975 --> 01:03:49.093
&gt;&gt; I think we have time for
one more question.

01:03:49.094 --> 01:03:50.983
We actually have a talk
starting at three, so

01:03:50.984 --> 01:03:52.991
we have to be out of the room
by a quarter to, so.

01:03:52.992 --> 01:03:54.641
[LAUGH] One more and then we'll-
&gt;&gt; Great.

01:03:54.642 --> 01:03:56.970
&gt;&gt; I do if no one else does.

01:03:56.971 --> 01:04:00.310
So you mentioned that
Thought Challenger.

01:04:00.311 --> 01:04:03.504
You had very good results
with engagement and

01:04:03.505 --> 01:04:05.951
usage over your
eight week trial.

01:04:05.952 --> 01:04:10.230
And you said you didn't
think that the coaching was-

01:04:10.231 --> 01:04:11.700
&gt;&gt; All of it, yeah.

01:04:11.701 --> 01:04:12.979
&gt;&gt; Was responsible for it.

01:04:12.980 --> 01:04:14.379
&gt;&gt; Well, sorry.
I don't think the coaching was

01:04:14.380 --> 01:04:15.769
all of the engagement,
yeah, sure.

01:04:15.770 --> 01:04:17.545
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so what was it about,

01:04:17.546 --> 01:04:20.596
what future do you think-
&gt;&gt; I think one of the reasons is

01:04:20.597 --> 01:04:23.870
that they were really designed
for very simple interactions.

01:04:23.871 --> 01:04:26.229
They were meant to be brief.

01:04:26.230 --> 01:04:29.521
That a lot of these other
applications we see tend to be

01:04:29.522 --> 01:04:30.800
multi-featured.

01:04:30.801 --> 01:04:33.810
So you'd have the thought
challenging piece in there with

01:04:33.811 --> 01:04:36.290
an activity scheduling piece,
and a logging piece, and

01:04:36.291 --> 01:04:37.600
some psycho-educational piece.

01:04:37.601 --> 01:04:40.138
And so you have to really
dig into these applications

01:04:40.139 --> 01:04:41.761
to get the thing that's useful.

01:04:41.762 --> 01:04:44.301
And so I think that sort
of simple design of these

01:04:44.302 --> 01:04:47.477
applications is actually sort
of useful to kind of increase

01:04:47.478 --> 01:04:48.250
engagement.

01:04:48.251 --> 01:04:50.650
And I think that was also shown
through at least the way that

01:04:50.651 --> 01:04:52.760
people engage in these things
in kind of quick small bursts.

01:04:52.761 --> 01:04:54.363
So I think that was
one piece of it.

01:04:54.364 --> 01:04:57.735
I also think that because it's
an app suite as opposed to

01:04:57.736 --> 01:05:01.177
a single application, I think
one of the problems I think

01:05:01.178 --> 01:05:04.120
that mobile mental health
faces is that people,

01:05:04.121 --> 01:05:07.708
when they go looking for
something, they're usually more

01:05:07.709 --> 01:05:11.180
highly motivated than they
are gonna be at other things.

01:05:11.181 --> 01:05:13.103
They might find an application,
they might start using it.

01:05:13.104 --> 01:05:14.157
It might not work, and

01:05:14.158 --> 01:05:16.125
they might then decide
to stop doing it.

01:05:16.126 --> 01:05:19.060
And they might have a thought
like, I tried that digital

01:05:19.061 --> 01:05:20.365
mental health stuff and
it doesn't work.

01:05:20.366 --> 01:05:22.596
And I think by reframing
this as an app sweep,

01:05:22.597 --> 01:05:25.261
there's is a little bit of
a reframe that can happen.

01:05:25.262 --> 01:05:27.434
That we know not everything
works for everyone and

01:05:27.435 --> 01:05:29.880
that's why we've made out
these different options.

01:05:29.881 --> 01:05:33.410
And so if the first thing you
use doesn't work for you,

01:05:33.411 --> 01:05:34.960
maybe something within
the suite will.

01:05:34.961 --> 01:05:38.480
So what we find in a lot of
other areas of psychology when

01:05:38.481 --> 01:05:41.200
we had these sort of multi-modal
sort of stuff is that these

01:05:42.690 --> 01:05:46.110
kitchen sink approaches work
much better than approaches that

01:05:46.111 --> 01:05:48.620
have sort of a single
piece to it.

01:05:48.621 --> 01:05:51.082
I mean, so I think by sort of
unpacking the kitchen sink and

01:05:51.083 --> 01:05:52.994
laying out the tools
kind of all separately,

01:05:52.995 --> 01:05:55.175
it kind of helps people
engage with that platform.

01:05:55.176 --> 01:05:59.062
That's just sort
of my conjecture.

01:05:59.063 --> 01:06:00.721
We don't have a lot of
evidence speaking to that.

01:06:00.722 --> 01:06:04.310
But I think that that's a thesis
kind of digging into it.

01:06:04.311 --> 01:06:06.210
But we need to do more
research to figure that out.

01:06:06.211 --> 01:06:07.900
It's a good question, cool.

01:06:07.901 --> 01:06:08.954
&gt;&gt; All right,
let's thank [CROSSTALK].

01:06:08.955 --> 01:06:18.954
&gt;&gt; Thanks, everybody.
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE].

